function e(e){return e.filter(e=>!!e.marker).map(e=>{let t=e.text.indexOf(`. `),n=t>0?e.text.slice(0,t+1):e.text,r=n.length>60?`${n.slice(0,60).replace(/[\s,;:]+$/,``)}…`:n;return{key:e.marker,text:`${e.marker}) ${r}`}})}var t={id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp`,title:`长篇阅读（信息匹配）`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Michael Wardian pushed forward into the penetrating arctic (北极的) wind, fighting the urge to speed up. Too much effort and he'd begin to sweat, which, he was told, would only increase the risk of hypothermia (体温过低).`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`At the 2014 North Pole Marathon, the temperature dipped to minus-22 degrees F, with a wind chill that made it feel even colder. Along the route, armed guards wandered the large sheets of floating ice to minimize the risk of polar bear attacks.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`"I like to do stuff that scares me," Wardian said. With ice frozen to his beard, Wardian crossed the finish line that April afternoon in a winning time of 4 hours 7 minutes and 40 seconds, almost two hours slower than his personal best over 26.2 miles. The race for Wardian, however, was less about the result than overcoming his aversion to the cold.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`In a few days, Wardian will once again compete in an unfamiliar territory and below-freezing temperatures. He will line up Monday in Antarctica (南极) for the first leg of the World Marathon Challenge—joining 32 other adventure seekers on an unusual journey where participants travel through different time zones and climates to run seven marathons on seven continents in seven days.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`An elite ultra-runner, Wardian has his sights on breaking the event's record average marathon time of 3:32:25 set last January by U. S. Marine Corps captain Daniel Cartica. Wardian, a 42-year-old Arlington resident, is a record-breaking racer, known in the ultra-running community for seeking tough courses and setting world records. Last year, he ran 1,254. 65 miles in 47 races. The World Marathon Challenge, like most of Wardian's running goals, will be about pushing his limits. "I love diverse and unique challenges," he said. "I'm definitely interested in seeing what I can handle and what my body can accept. That drives me."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`Something about the way Richard Donovan carried himself appealed to Wardian. Perhaps it was the sense of adventure Donovan displayed when they first met at the 2010 50K Championships in Galway, Ireland, where Donovan was the race director. The two hit it off, and soon Wardian was participating in Donovan's events. It was at the North Pole Marathon, a race that Donovan organizes, that Wardian first heard about the Irishman's plan for the World Marathon Challenge- a challenge that Donovan himself completed in 2009 and 2012. "I knew that many people had a goal of running seven marathons on seven continents during any time period," Donovan, 50, said. "I felt the natural extension to this idea would be to try to achieve it within a seven-day period."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`Wardian started saving for the trip in 2014, connecting with sponsors and getting approval from his wife, Jennifer, before committing. Registration for the event costs 36, 000 euros, which covers international charter flights to each of the seven marathon locations: Union Glacier (Antarctica), Punta Arenas, Chile (South America), Miami (North America), Madrid (Europe), Marrakesh, Morocco (Africa), Dubai (Asia) and Sydney (Australia). The challenge is a test of both physical strength and mental fitness. Sleeping on a crammed plane, adjusting to different time zones and finding food to eat (Wardian is a vegetarian) would make it an exhausting trip over a month, let alone a week. "The key to a race like this is getting comfortable being uncomfortable," said Becca Pizzi, last year's women's champion. "The highs of the race are incredibly high, and the lows incredibly low."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`Since turning it into an organized event in 2015, Donovan has attracted a variety of runners. This year's challenge will feature a far more elite field, which includes Ryan Hall, America's fastest marathon runner. Despite his proven track record, Hall said he has no time goals and that he still suffers from the same fatigue issues that forced him to leave the professional ranks in 2015. Hall plans to run with his friend, Pastor Matthew Barnett of The Dream Center in Los Angeles- one of the six American men who will be competing. "I don't expect to run a step with Mike, but I will be excited to see how he does," said the 34-year-old Hall, who began weight-lifting after retiring. "If I finish within an hour of him in each marathon, I'd be surprised."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Instead, 43-year-old Petr Vabrousek, an elite Czech Ironman champion, is expected to be Wardian's closest challenger. To others on the trip, simply finishing will be its own reward. Sinead Kane of Ireland is aiming to become the first blind person to complete the challenge. And Beth Ann Telford, a 47-year-old federal government worker from Fairfax and the only American female in this year's mix, is using the event as a platform to raise money for cancer research. It's a cause with a personal connection to Telford, who was diagnosed with brain cancer in 2004. "Doing something Hke this is definitely the hardest challenge that I've ever done except for the chemotherapy and brain surgery," she said. "It's going to raise awareness... I just wanted to do something that is epic and this certainly is right up there." Wardian, too, hopes his involvement will give him a platform to promote a cause. He recently became an ambassador for the United Nations Women's HeForShe initiative to fight inequalities faced by women and girls worldwide.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`On a chilly December afternoon, Wardian wove through Washington's crowded sidewalks on the way home from his full-time job as an international ship broker. His elastic, 6-foot frame bounced gently and efficiently off the ground with each step of the hilly six-mile trip back to Arlington. This is a daily routine during the week for Wardian, who started racing professionally in 2003 and runs seven days a week, often multiple times a day. When he travels, he prefers to explore new places on his feet.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`But in some ways, Wardian still has trouble thinking of himself as a runner. For the majority of his childhood, Wardian devoted his energy to becoming a Division I lacrosse (长曲棍球) player- a dream he realized when he was recruited to play at Michigan State University. "Once he decides to do something, he just works at it until he does it," Michaefs younger sister, Mariele, said. "Once he decides to do it, it's usually something that's going to happen. He's always been like that. He's a very motivated individual."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`It was only a year or so ago that Wardian realized that he had been a runner longer than a lacrosse player. It was not until he ran in the 2004 U. S. Olympic Marathon Trials -— the first of three for Wardian -- that he felt that he was a legitimate runner. Now more than 10 years and numerous ultra marathon national titles and world records later, he embraces that identity. Wardian wants to see how far his legs can take him, one epic challenge at a time. "I want to always keep doing things that are exciting, adventurous, different and most importantly, probably things I'm not the best at," Wardian said, "because if you're not seeking things out that are challenging and difficult for you, then you're not growing... So I hope maybe people see what I do, and say, 'Okay, I want to do something different or try something new ... I'm going to do something that scares me.' That's what I'm hoping people will take from it."`}]},n={id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp`,title:`长篇阅读（信息匹配）`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`What do we lose when natural spaces and species disappear? Increasingly, research has shown that as species and ecosystems vanish, it also chips away at our ability to preserve what remains -- because we no longer understand what we're losing.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`You probably see it all the time. The neighbor who puts pesticides on his lawn rather than deal with annoying bees. The politician who votes against wildlife protection because she's never seen a wolf in the wild. The corporation that wants to bulldoze (用推土机推平) the habitat of a rare frog.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`At best this can be termed "the extinction of experience," where our cultural and natural histories fade from our memories and therefore our reality. At its worst it becomes something even more concerning: "biophobia," the fear of living things and a complete aversion to nature.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`This isn't the fiction of living in a cold, empty dystopia (绝望的世界). Sadly, it's becoming a way of life for too many people—especially children. A recent study in Japan paints a striking portrait of this problem. A survey of more than 5, 300 school children in the Tochigi Prefecture examined their perception of 14 local insect species and one spider. The results? A collective "ew!" Most of the students saw the species as things to dislike or fear, or even as sources of danger. The less experience the students had with nature, the more negative their feelings.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`The results were published earlier this year in the journal Biological Conservation. Lead researcher Masashi Soga with the University of Tokyo says the study stemmed from observations about today's nature-deficient children. "Humans inherently avoid dangerous organisms such as bees, but children these days avoid even harmless insects such as butterflies and dragonflies (蜻蜓),"he says. "I have long wondered why so many of today's children react like this."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`Although the children's reactions were somewhat expected, the new study did contain an unexpected finding: Many of the surveyed children revealed that their parents also expressed fear or disgust of the same animals. In fact these parental emotions were strong enough to overwhelm any positive experiences the children might have gained from direct experiences in nature. As Soga and his coauthors wrote in their paper, "Our results suggest that there is likely a feedback loop in which an increase in people who have negative attitudes towards nature in one generation will lead to a further increase in people with similar attitudes in the next generation."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`And that's possibly the greater threat posed by extinction of experience. Soga suggests the generational loss- a condition previously dubbed environmental generational amnesia (遗忘) -- could chip away at our societal ability to preserve what we're losing. "I believe that increased biophobia is a major, but invisible, threat to global biodiversity," Soga says. "As the number of children who have biophobia increases, public interest and support for biodiversity conservation will gradually decline. Although many conservation biologists still consider that preventing the loss of wildlife habitat is the most important way to conserve biodiversity, I think preventing increased biophobia is also important for conservation."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`What's to be done about this? The paper makes several recommendations, the most obvious of which is that children should experience nature more often. The authors also suggest establishing policies to guide these natural experiences and increasing educational programs about the natural world.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Helping parents to see species around them in a new light would make a difference, too. And, of course, maintaining support for preserving the wild spaces where these "scary" creatures live is the most important thing of all. That's a point reinforced by another recent study, which found that wild spaces located within urban areas—and the plants and animals that thrive in them—are particularly important for human health and well-being.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`Published in the journal Frontiers in Sustainable Cities, the study examined attitudes toward Discovery Park, the heavily forested 534-acre public park in Seattle, Washington. It found that the public had the most appreciation for -- and gained the most value from -- the wildest parts of the park. "I have seen whales, seals, fish, eagles, shorebirds and many other sea creatures in their natural habitat," one survey participant wrote. "Coming here with people has allowed me to connect and talk with them about conversation that simply does not happen in everyday life," wrote another.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`The participants reported that their most valuable experiences in the park included encountering wildlife, walking through open spaces, exploring the beach and finding beautiful views. "We saw that a large majority of participants' interactions, especially their most meaningful interactions, depended on Discovery Park's relative wildness," says lead author Elizabeth Lev, a master's student in the University of Washington's Human Interaction with Nature Lab. This is only possible because the park is relatively wild. After all, you can't enjoy watching birds if there are no birds to follow? gaze at the sunset if it's obscured by skyscrapers; or stop and smell the flowers if they don't have room to grow.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`And yet even this long-protected space could someday become less hospitable to nature. Over the past few years a lot of people and organizations have suggested developing parts of Discovery Park or the neighboring area. Most recently a plan proposed building 34 acres of much-needed affordable housing and parking spaces adjacent to the park, bringing with them noise, traffic and pollution.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-M`,marker:`M`,text:`If anything like that happened, both the park and the people of Seattle could lose something vital. And that would continue the trend of chipping away at Seattle's—and the world's—natural spaces, leaving just tiny pocket parks and green-but-empty spaces that offer little real value to wildlife, plants or people.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-N`,marker:`N`,text:`"It is true that any interaction with nature is better than none, but I don't want people to be satisfied with any small bit of grass and trees," Lev says. "We have been in this cycle of environmental generational amnesia for a long time, where the baseline keeps shifting and we don't even realize what we're losing until it's gone. If we can get people to understand how much meaning and value can come from having more experiences with more wild forms of nature, then maybe we can stop this cycle and move toward conserving and restoring what we have left."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-O`,marker:`O`,text:`Building this understanding in an ever-more fearful and disconnected world may be the biggest challenge. Peter Kahn, the senior author of Lev's paper and the director of the Human Interaction with Nature Lab, made several suggestions for bridging this gap in this 2011 book, Technological Nature. They echo the recommendation about getting children into nature, but also include telling stories of how things used to be, imagining what things might be like in the future, and developing a common language about nature, "a way of speaking about wild and domestic interaction patterns, and the meaningful, deep and often joyful feelings that they generate."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-mp-P`,marker:`P`,text:`No matter what techniques we use, this growing field of research illustrates that saving nature requires encouraging people to experience it more often and more deeply. That calls for additional research -- Lev and her coauthors have published a toolkit that other municipalities can follow to study the value of their own wild spaces -- and clear communication of the results. "If we can continue to show people the benefits of these wild spaces," Lev says, "maybe people will begin to see more value in keeping these areas undeveloped- for the sake of our mutual benefit."`}]},r={id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp`,title:`长篇阅读（信息匹配）`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`In 1913, classical music sparked a riot in Paris. Igor Stravinsky was introducing his revolutionary "Rite of Spring" ballet to the world, with its discordant melodies and unorthodox choreography (编舞), and the purists in the crowd expressed their disapproval loud and clear. It might have been classical music's version of the time Bob Dylan went electric at the Newport Folk Festival. "The noise, fighting, and shouting in the audience got so loud," NPR's music reporter Miles Hoffman said of the Stravinsky debut, "that the choreographer had to shout out the numbers to the dancers so that they knew what they were supposed to do."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`It's difficult to imagine a similar disturbance occurring today within America's sacred symphony halls. In fact, it's hard to picture any kind of disruptive activity at all (unless someone's cell phone happens to go off, and then you'd better watch your back). A mannerly aura (氛围) hangs over most classical proceedings, and many of the genre's biggest supporters would have it no other way.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`Today, Western audiences for classical music and opera and ballet are almost always well dressed, older, respectful, achingly silent and often very wealthy (one has to be able to afford most tickets). But as many of America's most storied "highbrow" (高雅的) institutions struggle financially -- the Philadelphia Orchestra's much-publicized rebound from bankruptcy is just one recent example -- classical music fans and theorists are wondering how the medium can weave itself into the 21st century's cultural fabric without sacrificing its integrity.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`For example, should we feel OK "clapping" during classical music events, even if nobody else is? Why shouldn't we cheer for something great, like we do at a rock concert? The Huffington Post recently ran a Great Debate on this issue and many commenters came out on the side of silence. "There is no more rewarding experience in life than being part of an audience where everybody is leaning forward in silence, thoroughly carried away by a great performance of a masterpiece," one commenter wrote. "Why is it so difficult for folks to develop an appreciation and understanding for the mannerisms and traditions of classical music?" asked another.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`The truth is that classical music audiences weren't always so polite. Robert Greenberg, an award-winning composer, said that when Beethoven first performed his 7th Symphony, audiences forced the orchestra to perform encores (重演) of certain movements immediately, applauding wildly. And in the last few decades, he said, many audiences at opera performances have abandoned pretenses, yelling "Bravo" when they feel like it.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`"I don't think there's anything wrong with an audience showing their enthusiasm for a proper moment by applauding, showing their joy," Greenberg said, noting that the stuffiness in concert halls is "one aspect of contemporary concert etiquette" he doesn't understand. "Instead of waiting half an hour to show enthusiasm, why not show it every eight or nine minutes?"`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`Until the rules about behavior and clothing change, it's hard to imagine multitudes of young people filling concert halls on their own accord. They're probably more likely to head to Central Park to watch a free performance with a bottle of wine and their friends. "I think anyone should be able to come into a performance dressed any way they like, and be comfortable any way they like, sitting in that seat ready to enjoy themselves," Greenberg said. "Because it's enjoyable."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`Greenberg stressed that he doesn't want people to start respecting the music less, and he's not suggesting that we "dumb down" the experience. Rather, it's about opening up "access". When operas first instituted subtitles (字幕) during shows, he said, many purists didn't like the idea, believing that the audience should instead study the works before attending. But now it's commonplace to find titles on the seatback in front of you—choose a language, sit back, and understand what's going on.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Allison Vulgamore, president of the Philadelphia Orchestra, is certainly looking to the future. She says certain "classics concerts" dedicated to the old masters will always exist, but not every program has to feature Beethoven and Brahms—or even a stage and seats. "We're trying to introduce different kinds of concerts in different ways," she said. "We are an interactive society now, where people like to learn."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`As the Philadelphia Orchestra rebounds from its financial straits, it is also aiming to experiment, without alienating the loyalists. Vulgamore pointed to Cirque de la Symphonic, a recent offering in which jugglers (玩杂耍的人) and acrobats (杂技演员) interacted with musicians. An upcoming collaboration with New York City's Ridge Theatre, meanwhile, will feature a "suspended dance installation" and other theatrical elements occurring in conjunction with an orchestral piece.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`The orchestra also continues to offer $25 annual memberships to Philadelphia students, who can buy rush tickets to every concert on the schedule. "Students line up for the concerts they want, and we get roughly 300 or 350 kids a night coming to these. They take any of the open seats available, 5 minutes before the concert starts," Vulgamore said. "It's like the running of the bulls, that energy when the doors open."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`Greenberg thinks that youthful energy needs to be harvested. Conductors don't have to be arrogant and untouchable- they can be accessible. Perhaps there could even be a "bit of humor" about them, he suggested, and an abandoning of pretension within the high-art institutions themselves. "On one hand, these organizations are all saying the same thing: we want more general audiences, to break down cultural barriers," he said. "But then they come up with some very snooty (目中无人的) thing that makes you crazy."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-M`,marker:`M`,text:`John Terauds, a critic who has covered Toronto's classical music scene extensively, also wants to do away with the stuffiness. He suggested that the warmer an audience is, the better the musicians themselves will respond. "But the producer or organizer has to let everyone know it's OK," he said. "It's OK to enjoy yourself." At the Toronto Symphony Orchestra, for example, conductor Peter Oundjian often stops between pieces, taking a moment to talk about the composer or the music in a very amiable way. And some nights, Terauds said, "at least a third" of the audience consists of students who have purchased cheaper tickets. On these nights, the energy of the room drastically shifts. It becomes a less intimidating place.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-N`,marker:`N`,text:`Back in February, Terauds wrote on his blog about how going to classical performances can be intimidating. Certain people "think they have to dress up," he wrote. "They think they have to know something about the music before they go. And, I'm sure, sitting in a seat, trembling in fear that this might be the wrong time to applaud, is also one of the factors."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-O`,marker:`O`,text:`Everyone in the classical world agrees on the need for increased "accessibility," but achieving it is often easier said than done. Nowadays, there are unknown, unorthodox opera singers wowing (博得...的喝彩) viewers on TV programs like "America's Got Talent" and "The Voice". What can higher institutions do with any of that? And if they appeal to these outlets, do they risk compromising the integrity or the intelligence of the music?`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-mp-P`,marker:`P`,text:`Vulgamore seems to understand this. She thinks an organization can have it both ways, claiming the new while keeping the old. And as she reorganizes the Philadelphia Orchestra, she will attempt to do just that. "The world's most respected musicians brought together as an orchestra will always exist," she said. "But it's essential that we be willing to experiment and fail."`}]},i={"cet6-2022-12-s1":{parts:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "With the application of information technology in education, college students can now learn in more diverse and efficient ways." You can make statements, give reasons, or cite examples to develop your essay.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`With the application of information technology in education, college students can now learn in more diverse and efficient ways. This advancement has revolutionized the traditional classroom setting, making learning more interactive and accessible.
Firstly, the integration of IT in education has enabled students to access a vast array of resources online. They can now engage with digital libraries, participate in virtual discussions, and utilize educational apps that cater to their individual learning styles. Moreover, the use of video conferencing tools has facilitated real-time interaction with peers and professors from around the globe, fostering a more collaborative learning environment.
In conclusion, the advent of information technology in education has not only enhanced the learning experience but also prepared students for the digital age. As we continue to innovate, the potential for further improvements in educational technology is limitless, promising a future where learning is truly personalized and globally connected.`,guidance:[`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "With the application of information technology in education, college students can now learn in more diverse and effic…。参考范文约 148 词、3 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,`范文结构线索：Firstly / Moreover / In conclusion——这些衔接语标示了论点展开与收束的顺序，可作为自己写作时的段落框架参考。`,`自评建议：对照题目要求逐项检查——是否回应全部提问、段落之间是否有明确逻辑连接、句式与词汇是否匹配话题、词数是否落在规定区间。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "With the application of information technology in education, college students can now learn in more diverse and efficient ways." You can make statements, give reasons, or cite examples to develop your essay.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "With the application of information technology in education, college students can now learn in more diverse and effic…。参考范文约 148 词、3 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,score:15}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`How's your dissertation going? I'm proofreading my first draft, and will submit it to my professor tomorrow. Oh, I haven't even started writing mine yet, so I'm really worried about finishing by the end of next semester. You mean you haven't even begun yours yet? The final draft is due in five months. Of course I've started it, but I can't get to the writing yet, as I haven't found enough resources to use, so I'm still researching the topic. Maybe the problem is the way you're doing your research. I started by talking to my professor about where to look for information, and based on that, I found books in the library and a lot of reputable journal articles on the Internet. I've tried all that, but don't have enough to write the dissertation, as my department's minimum length is 70 pages. I think the problem is that my topic isn't viable, and honestly, my professor did warn me at the beginning that I might not be able to find enough material, but I was so interested in the topic that I didn't let his advice deter me. Well, I suggest you find a new topic. After all, our professors are here to guide us, so it's best to listen to them. In retrospect, I wish I had listened to him. But I didn't, and now I don't want to give up my topic, as I've already invested so much time and energy. If you're committed to your current topic, maybe you could make some adjustments, rather than abandoning it completely. What is your topic? It's Depictions of Femininity in Folklore from the South of the Country. That's pretty narrow. You could find more material if you made the topic broader, maybe by including other kinds of depictions. Broadening the topic is a great idea. I'll start by including folklore from other regions of the country.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`Today on BookTalk, we're lucky enough to host John Robbins and discuss his new book, Why Americans Are Fat and How We Can Lose Weight. John isn't just a respected writer, he's also one of the rare celebrity authors writing about science today. Thanks for having me, Rebecca, but I'm hardly a celebrity. That's very modest of you to say, considering that your four books have sold a total of 7 million copies worldwide, and they've been translated into 12 different languages. What makes people so fascinated with your work? Well, people read my books because more than 60% of Americans are overweight or obese, and other countries are facing similar problems. Basically, we all want to know how to fix things. We certainly do. I've read your new book, and it's fabulous, especially when it comes to the way you make difficult science easy for laymen to understand. That's no small achievement. I'm glad to hear you find my work accessible, because I was worried when I wrote it that discussing the science might make the book more suited for a specialist audience. My last book was written primarily for the medical community, but this time, I want to help ordinary people take control of their weight. And how do you suggest they do that? Can you give us the basics of your advice for people who want to lose weight? Briefly, I argue that every person needs to consider their metabolism and eat what suits their body's needs. I don't advocate one single diet. Some people should eat more carbohydrates than others, and different people need different amounts of protein and fat. But you do have some recommendations for everyone, including eating 10 servings of vegetables and 3 of fruit a day. We'll talk about those recommendations next, but now we need to take a short break for a message from our sponsor.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-ls-3`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:9,text:`Stress is often depicted as negative, but research shows that moderate amounts of it can be beneficial for your brain and your body. First, the benefits for the brain. Studies have shown that short periods of stress can actually bolster cognitive functioning. Researchers discovered that placing rats in a stressful situation for just a few hours doubled the growth of new brain cells. The rats also did better on a memory test later on. Scientists think the same thing happens in humans. But how does stress improve memory? It's simple. When your brain cells multiply, your memory can improve. Viewed from a biological perspective, this makes sense, because animals that are better at remembering dangerous situations can avoid them in the future. If an animal encounters a predator and escapes, for example, it's important to remember where and when that encounter happened. Experts assert that the same principle applies to humans. Now, let's turn to how stress benefits the body. This may come as a surprise to laymen, but experts say that stress can keep you from getting sick. Scientists concede that chronic stress can make you more prone to illness, but research shows that short periods of stress can actually provide some protection against getting sick, because it increases your immune functioning. One study shows that rats that experienced brief stress had a surge of immune cell response, which makes the immune system better prepared to fight illness. For humans, there is even evidence that experiencing stress before getting vaccinated could help make vaccines more effective.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-ls-4`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:19,text:`For many managers and people who work in leadership positions, dealing with emails is a dilemma. It's likely the unpredictable, uncontrollable, and ongoing nature of day-to-day email in terms of volume, importance, and urgency contributes to their levels of anxiety and to diminished leadership skills. That's because it's not unusual for many leaders to prioritize email management over people management. An obsession with managing their inbox prevents them from dealing with their employees. As a result, they ignore the issues that might only be mild problems at first, until, unfortunately, they inevitably transform into a major problem or crisis by virtue of neglect. As leaders, they are expected to motivate and inspire their team in pursuit of longer-term strategic goals, and also, less ambitiously but more practically, to monitor their daily output, to set clear expectations, and to give regular feedback. When presented with a choice between the appeal of their inbox and other more important activities, many sacrifice the latter. Daily email demands have a negative impact on their goal progress. This is because leaders must divert resources from other tasks to check, filter, and respond to emails. The solution is cultivating self-control, which is like a muscle. It can be strengthened or improved over time through exercise. Some suggestions include making space in your diary for the only periods during which you'll be checking emails, setting a timer for yourself so you don't become distracted by your inbox for too long, turning off email alerts so you're not interrupted by them. What are leaders expected to do in pursuing their strategic goals? In last week's lecture, we talked about the problems caused by poor eating habits, focusing on how medical professionals are trying to solve these problems. Today, we'll continue with the topic, but focus on research from a different field. Marketing. Now, what can marketing tell us about improving nutrition? Well, a team of marketing professors has studied the importance of the visual aspect of food, and how that influences food choices. Those marketing experts assert that the impact of the appearance of food is greater than we might presume, and might hold the key to encouraging better eating. So, how important is the appearance of food? Research shows that just seeing an appealing photograph of a hamburger in an advertisement, for example, can cause individuals to imagine the taste or smell of that hamburger. This can make them more likely to purchase and consume it. Of course, that's the point of advertisements, so isn't that a good thing? Well, the marketing professors argue that this is actually a problem, because the combination of pleasing aesthetics of and easy access to unhealthy foods, such as hamburgers and pizza, may be contributing to a worldwide health crisis. In fact, statistics show that 39% of all adults in the world weigh too much, and another 13% are obese. Now, as we discussed last time, medical professionals have tried to fight the obesity epidemic by focusing on the nutrients found in different foods, emphasizing things like carbohydrate, fat, sugar, and calorie content. But they've had limited success. The marketing professors believe that the real solution to obesity is making food look appealing and focusing on the pleasure of both looking at and eating that food. That pleasure can be used as a tool to promote healthy food choices. In fact, the research done by the team showed that associating healthy food with pleasant images, experiences, and emotions led to greater interest in purchasing or eating it. This is a better strategy, they claim, than reminding consumers that a certain food is good for them. But are they right? Well, the researchers cited a marketing campaign designed to reverse the sales decline of carrots. The campaign didn't emphasize the carrots' healthy qualities, but embrace their beauty and the pleasure derived from eating them. For example, the ads focused on the bright, beautiful orange color and crisp texture of the carrots. The campaign led to an impressive increase in product sales of more than 10%. The researchers believe that other companies could bolster sales of healthy foods with similar ads, depicting their products as attractive and a source of pleasure. The household cleaning products industry really began to take off in the 1950s. Prior to that, resourceful housewives who had grown up in a less prosperous era relied on substances that they used in their kitchens, such as vinegar used to clean windows. Today, the household cleaning products industry is worth billions of dollars a year and is dominated by large global companies. In recent years, however, many countries have witnessed a surge in the number of small businesses and companies offering more environment-friendly cleaning products. And they're doing good business, too. One reason for this is that consumers are becoming more aware of environment and sustainability issues. They also want to make their home a safe place, free from toxic chemicals. The third reason is people's awareness of and knowledge about technology has never been greater. In the past, people might have been skeptical of a new product claimed to be able to achieve amazing cleaning results. But they now have faith in technology's ability to do what was once thought impossible. There is perhaps no better example of this than a patented cloth sold by one company which remarkably uses only water rather than traditional cleaning products. The company claims its cloth lifts, traps, and removes dirt and bacteria. And considering that people don't see these products in shops or advertised on TV or in the press, it has been a pretty steep rise. Many of the people behind these companies began testing the viability of their products by selling them at local markets. Then, in many cases, customers are doing the marketing for these products by leaving threads of comments on social media praising their virtues. There are also TV shows and books dedicated to maintaining home tidiness and cleanliness and the benefits of having a clean home. Having a safe and clean home might be one thing people feel they can control in an increasingly out-of-control world. And there are social media celebrities. One woman in Britain has become a social media sensation with more than two million followers for her cleaning tips page.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-ls-5`,label:`Passage 3`,questionNo:22,text:`Throwing spare change into a fountain is a time-honored ritual. Throw a penny into the water, and your wish might come true. But all that money has to go somewhere. Otherwise, the growing piles of pennies, quarters, and euros could clog up the fountain's works. Today, I'm going to talk about where all the coins go. Well, the coins collected can go to all sorts of different places, from fountain maintenance to charity or public service. In New York City, for example, coins collected from fountains in public parks often go towards the fountain's maintenance itself, though entrepreneurs who don't mind getting their hands wet often get to it first. There are over 50 beautiful, decorative display fountains in New York City parks. They are cleaned by the park's staff every few weeks. But most of the coins have already been removed by entrepreneurial New Yorkers, and there is not a significant amount left to be collected. Other cities, though, can pull in a much more serious haul. Take, for example, Rome's famous Trevi Fountain. For hundreds of years, visitors have thrown coins over their shoulder into the fountain to ensure that they will return. So many tourists toss in coins that Roman officials have the fountain cleaned every night, reportedly getting as much as $4,000 in loose change from around the world each day. Most of the money collected each night goes towards running a supermarket for the needy, and collecting that cash is serious business. Roman officials have been known to be tough on anyone caught skimming coins from the fountain. In one case in 2005, police arrested four fountain cleaners after they were spotted slipping coins into their own pockets after collecting them. Authorities finally caught one notorious thief named Thomas Morgan and banned him from the fountain after he fished out thousands of dollars in change over 34 years using a magnetic stick. For the most part, money collected from privately owned fountains in the United States also goes to charity. The fountain in New York City's Bryant Park is owned and operated by a not-for -profit corporation, which puts the cash collected by cleaners towards the fountain's own maintenance. Tens of thousands of dollars in coins removed from wishing wells, fountains, and ponds in Florida's Walt Disney World are donated each year to support foster children living in the state. Whether or not your wish comes true after tossing a coin into a fountain, you can rest assured knowing that the change is likely going to someone who needs it.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say about her dissertation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`She hasn't started writing it.`},{key:`B`,text:`She hasn't decided on a topic.`},{key:`C`,text:`She is proofreading the first draft.`},{key:`D`,text:`She is working on the references.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："Oh, I haven't even started writing mine yet, so I'm really worried about finishing by the end of next semester."。正确选项为 "She hasn't started writing it."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about his professor?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`He lent many books to the man for reference.`},{key:`B`,text:`He offered the man advice on resource hunting.`},{key:`C`,text:`He published a lot in a number of reputable journals.`},{key:`D`,text:`He told the man to be selective when using e-resources.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："I think the problem is that my topic isn't viable, and honestly, my professor did warn me at the beginning that I might not be able to find enough material, but I was so interested in the topic that I didn't let his advice deter me."。正确选项为 "He offered the man advice on resource hunting."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say about her professor?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`He didn't think her dissertation topic viable.`},{key:`B`,text:`He wasn't interested in her dissertation topic.`},{key:`C`,text:`He didn't want her to rush through her dissertation.`},{key:`D`,text:`He wasn't specific about the length of her dissertation.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："I think the problem is that my topic isn't viable, and honestly, my professor did warn me at the beginning that I might not be able to find enough material, but I was so interested in the topic that I didn't let his advice deter me."。正确选项为 "He didn't think her dissertation topic viable."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn the woman will do to complete her dissertation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Change her research methodology.`},{key:`B`,text:`Narrow down her dissertation topic.`},{key:`C`,text:`Consult her professor more.`},{key:`D`,text:`Follow the man's advice.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "Follow the man's advice."。可在听力原文（Conversation 1）中回听定位。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say about the man in her introduction?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`He has translated 12 books.`},{key:`B`,text:`He is a well-known nutritionist.`},{key:`C`,text:`His books sell well worldwide.`},{key:`D`,text:`His latest book sold a million copies.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："That's very modest of you to say, considering that your four books have sold a total of 7 million copies worldwide, and they've been translated into 12 different languages."。正确选项为 "His books sell well worldwide."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What has motivated the man to write his books?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The desire of Americans to try exotic cuisines.`},{key:`B`,text:`The demand for information about food safety.`},{key:`C`,text:`The fact that over half of Americans are overweight.`},{key:`D`,text:`The fact that science books are difficult to read.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："Well, people read my books because more than 60% of Americans are overweight or obese, and other countries are facing similar problems."。正确选项为 "The fact that over half of Americans are overweight."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`Who does the man say his last book was mainly written for?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The general public.`},{key:`B`,text:`Those who are overweight.`},{key:`C`,text:`Those who want to lose weight.`},{key:`D`,text:`The medical community.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："My last book was written primarily for the medical community, but this time, I want to help ordinary people take control of their weight."。正确选项为 "The medical community."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man recommend people do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Switch to a vegetarian diet.`},{key:`B`,text:`Follow a personalized diet.`},{key:`C`,text:`Adhere to doctors' advice.`},{key:`D`,text:`Cut carbohydrate intake.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "Follow a personalized diet."。可在听力原文（Conversation 2）中回听定位。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What did researchers discover about rats placed briefly in a stressful situation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The rate of their growth increased dramatically.`},{key:`B`,text:`The growth of their new brain cells doubled.`},{key:`C`,text:`They began to show signs of depression.`},{key:`D`,text:`They began to get irritated and restless.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："Researchers discovered that placing rats in a stressful situation for just a few hours doubled the growth of new brain cells."。正确选项为 "The growth of their new brain cells doubled."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`Why do people tend to have clearer memories of dangerous situations they have encountered?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`To avoid them in the future.`},{key:`B`,text:`To warn others against them.`},{key:`C`,text:`To make good sense of them.`},{key:`D`,text:`To reflect on their causes.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："Viewed from a biological perspective, this makes sense, because animals that are better at remembering dangerous situations can avoid them in the future."。正确选项为 "To avoid them in the future."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What do scientists believe chronic stress can do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Produce a surprising healing effect.`},{key:`B`,text:`Weaken one's immunity in the long run.`},{key:`C`,text:`Make people more susceptible to illness.`},{key:`D`,text:`Provide protection against mental illnesses.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："Scientists concede that chronic stress can make you more prone to illness, but research shows that short periods of stress can actually provide some protection against getting sick, because it increases your immune functioning."。正确选项为 "Make people more susceptible to illness."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say is a common problem with managers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Placing their own interests over their staff.`},{key:`B`,text:`Being overwhelmed by their daily routines.`},{key:`C`,text:`Lacking the ability to relate to their staff.`},{key:`D`,text:`Spending too much time handling email.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "Spending too much time handling email."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`What may happen when managers ignore minor problems?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Their leadership may be challenged.`},{key:`B`,text:`Their companies may go bankrupt.`},{key:`C`,text:`Unexpected events may occur.`},{key:`D`,text:`Major problems may result.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "Major problems may result."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`What are leaders expected to do in pursuing their strategic goals?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Keep an eye on their employees.`},{key:`B`,text:`Motivate and inspire their team.`},{key:`C`,text:`Sacrifice some of the immediate goals.`},{key:`D`,text:`Have greater ambition in overall planning.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "Motivate and inspire their team."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`How can a manager best avoid being distracted by email? From the four choices, mark A, B, C, and D.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Cultivate self-control.`},{key:`B`,text:`Filter their email boxes.`},{key:`C`,text:`Respond only after work.`},{key:`D`,text:`Check only when necessary.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "Cultivate self-control."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say a team of marketing professors has studied?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The key to increasing healthy food supply.`},{key:`B`,text:`The best way to improve marketing research.`},{key:`C`,text:`The impact of advertisements on consumption.`},{key:`D`,text:`The importance of the appearance of food.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "The importance of the appearance of food."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`How have medical professionals tried to fight obesity?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`By focusing on the nutrients in different foods.`},{key:`B`,text:`By emphasizing the diversity of food.`},{key:`C`,text:`By stressing pleasing aesthetics of food.`},{key:`D`,text:`By winning the support of marketing professors.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "By focusing on the nutrients in different foods."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What can other companies learn from the example of the carrot promotion campaign?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They can attract customers with the healthy qualities of their products.`},{key:`B`,text:`They can boost sales of healthy foods by making them visually appealing.`},{key:`C`,text:`They can turn to marketing professors for advice.`},{key:`D`,text:`They can rely on advertising for sales promotion.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "They can boost sales of healthy foods by making them visually appealing."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about the cleaning products industry in recent years?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It has witnessed a spectacular surge in demand.`},{key:`B`,text:`It has met much criticism from environmentalists.`},{key:`C`,text:`It has seen more small businesses offering environment-friendly products.`},{key:`D`,text:`It has experienced increasingly fierce competition among global companies.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："In recent years, however, many countries have witnessed a surge in the number of small businesses and companies offering more environment-friendly cleaning products."。正确选项为 "It has seen more small businesses offering environment-friendly products."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`Why are newly developed cleaning products selling well?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Consumers now know much more about technology.`},{key:`B`,text:`Their mass production has sharply reduced the price.`},{key:`C`,text:`Consumers tend to favor all that is novel.`},{key:`D`,text:`Their quality has been greatly improved.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："The household cleaning products industry really began to take off in the 1950s."。正确选项为 "Consumers now know much more about technology."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`What are some customers doing to help promote non-traditional cleaning products?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Purchasing only this kind of products for home cleaning.`},{key:`B`,text:`Writing positive comments about them on social media.`},{key:`C`,text:`Demonstrating on TV how effective these products are.`},{key:`D`,text:`Telling one another about their incomparable virtues.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："Then, in many cases, customers are doing the marketing for these products by leaving threads of comments on social media praising their virtues."。正确选项为 "Writing positive comments about them on social media."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`What problem might be caused by the growing piles of coins in fountains?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Increasing cleaners' workload.`},{key:`B`,text:`Blocking the fountains, works.`},{key:`C`,text:`Breaking a time-honored ritual.`},{key:`D`,text:`Polluting the fountains' water.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 3）依据："Otherwise, the growing piles of pennies, quarters, and euros could clog up the fountain's works."。正确选项为 "Blocking the fountains, works."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about the coins collected from New York City's park fountains?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are occasionally retrieved by curious tourists.`},{key:`B`,text:`They are regularly donated to charity organizations.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are mostly used for the fountains' maintenance.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are usually used as wages for fountain cleaners.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 3）依据："In New York City, for example, coins collected from fountains in public parks often go towards the fountain's maintenance itself, though entrepreneurs who don't mind getting their hands wet often get to it first."。正确选项为 "They are mostly used for the fountains' maintenance."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the money collected from Rome's Trevi Fountain?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is invested in a series of businesses.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is used exclusively for its maintenance.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is used to run a supermarket for the needy.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is estimated to be about $ 40,000 a month.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 3）依据："Most of the money collected each night goes towards running a supermarket for the needy, and collecting that cash is serious business."。正确选项为 "It is used to run a supermarket for the needy."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about Thomas Morgan? That's the end of Listening Comprehension.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`He was arrested for stealing money from four fountain cleaners.`},{key:`B`,text:`He was sentenced to 34 years' imprisonment.`},{key:`C`,text:`He collected rare coins from around the world.`},{key:`D`,text:`He stole a lot of money from a fountain with a magnetic stick.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 3）依据："Authorities finally caught one notorious thief named Thomas Morgan and banned him from the fountain after he fished out thousands of dollars in change over 34 years using a magnetic stick."。正确选项为 "He stole a lot of money from a fountain with a magnetic stick."。`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C), and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section. you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`access`,B:`apparent`,C:`barely`,D:`dedication`,E:`escorted`,F:`froze`,G:`incredible`,H:`indignant`,I:`network`,J:`overriding`,K:`peacefully`,L:`presumably`,M:`stifled`,N:`struck`,O:`terrifying`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-cp-1`,text:`During the summer, when I was a visiting poet at a residency out of state, an angry, confused woman wandered into my class and said: "I have three guns and I want to use them." We all I was dizzy with fear. The woman, who later turned out to be a schizophrenic (精神分裂症患者) without A few months later, crisis`}]},t,{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p1`,title:`Passage One`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p1-1`,text:`Many people associate their self-worth with their work. The more successful their career, the better they feel about themselves. Work-related self-esteem is therefore a worthy ideal to pursue with vigor, right? Well, not always. According to recent research, in which psychologists interviewed 370 full-time workers over a period of three weeks, the reality is a little more complicated. And it involves negative as well as positive consequences.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p1-2`,text:`It's natural to be drawn towards pleasure and to step away from pain. In the workplace, if that pleasure comes from a triumph which swells our self-respect, people will try to repeat the accomplishment. But repeating that accomplishment is often not realistic, which can lead to severe negative emotional consequences when it doesn't reoccur. This form of motivation is widely regarded as a negative type of motivation. It can hinder other more positive motivation types, such as completing a task purely because it's fulfilling or enjoyable.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p1-3`,text:`What consumes the employee instead is a pressing need to feel mighty and sure of themselves. They then take on only tasks and objectives which serve that ego-driven need. As a result, to avoid feelings of shame and worthlessness associated with failure, they extend themselves to such a degree that there's a subsequent adverse effect on their well-being. This internal pressure to succeed at all costs demands a lot of effort. It depletes their energy, culminating in disproportionate levels of damaging sentiment.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p1-4`,text:`Those negative emotions mount into heightened anxiety, impacting their ability to make the most of their personal life. Their desire to avoid feeling inferior ends up making them feel inferior when it comes to their diminished capacity for friendship and leisure. They end up dissatisfied both at work and outside of it.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p1-5`,text:`But thankfully, for those people compelled almost entirely by this specific form of motivation, the news isn't all bad, or bad at all. The study also discovered several positive outcomes that can actually outweigh the harmful ones. Though these types of employees are motivated by the desire to avoid negative consequences, they are also motivated by the excitement of pursuing emotional rewards. This excitement makes pursuing goals enjoyable and stimulates pleasure and pride that would result from success. An effect of the positive motivation is that it neutralizes the existence of negative motivation.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p1-6`,text:`Sure, it affects people's personal lives to what could be deemed an unhealthy extent, because leisure activities are often seen as a part of life that must be sacrificed to manage work and family demands. However, the way people feel about their work has less to do with whether they're motivated by the preservation of self-esteem but more with the fact that they're simply motivated.`}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p2`,title:`Passage Two`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p2-1`,text:`Degradation of the world's natural resources by humans is rapidly outpacing the planet's ability to absorb the damage, a recent UN environmental study has found. The study concludes that without radical action the level of prosperity that millions of people in the developed world count on will be impossible to maintain or extend to poorer countries.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p2-2`,text:`Water scarcity is the curse of some of the poorest regions on Earth, leaving developing countries increasingly unable to feed themselves, and causing hardship for millions of people. There appears little prospect of this desperate situation being remedied without radical action being taken. Water resources are under increasing threat from population growth, climate change, rapid urbanization, rising levels of consumption, and the degradation of lands that previously provided a natural replenishment (补充) of water resources.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p2-3`,text:`The rate of damage to the natural environment was found to increase globally, despite concerted efforts to persuade governments to take measures to improve the condition. "If current trends continue, and the world fails to improve patterns of production and consumption, then the state of the world's environment will continue to decline," warned UN executive director Achim Steiner.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p2-4`,text:`He said the tools for improving the environment for millions of people existed in developed countries, but were in danger of not being used.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p2-5`,text:`The study found that basic measures to tackle some of the key causes of environmental damage were still not being taken. These included measures to reduce air pollution, to control the damage to marine eco-systems, which can have a huge effect on fish stocks on which hundreds of millions of people depend; and to curb the degradation of land where modern agricultural methods were pursued without regard to the longer-term consequences.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p2-6`,text:`Despite the recent global agreement on cutting greenhouse gas emissions, global carbon output continues to rise. This will put a long-term strain on the ability of developing economics to feed their own people. Climate change is aggravated by the emissions of greenhouse gases from chemical and natural fertilisers used in agriculture which increased by more than a quarter between 2000 and 2010. Other problem areas identified in the report included glaciers, which provide vital water resources for millions of people, but which are shrinking as the climate warms.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-p2-7`,text:`In rich countries, these problems have built up over decades and centuries while economic growth was pursued at the expense of the environment. Subsequent efforts to remedy the environment have met with partial success. But in developing countries, the path of future development has more potential to change, which has encouraged international institutions to devise more sustainable growth pathways that are supposed both to alleviate poverty and preserve the environment.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`F`,explanation:`答案 F（froze）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 F。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`A`,explanation:`答案 A（access）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`E`,explanation:`答案 E（escorted）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`K`,explanation:`答案 K（peacefully）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 K。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`O`,explanation:`答案 O（terrifying）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 O。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`N`,explanation:`答案 N（struck）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 N。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`B`,explanation:`答案 B（apparent）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`C`,explanation:`答案 C（barely）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`I`,explanation:`答案 I（network）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 I。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`G`,explanation:`答案 G（incredible）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 G。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`Wardian regards the various extraordinary challenges as a test of his physical endurance.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`正确答案 E。E 段："An elite ultra-runner, Wardian has his sights on breaking the event's record average marathon time of 3:32:25 set last January by U."，与题干陈述 "Wardian regards the various extraordinary challenges as a test of his physical endurance." 信息对应，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`Wardian hopes his participation in the seven-day marathon series will contribute to a worthy cause.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`正确答案 I。I 段："" Wardian, too, hopes his involvement will give him a platform to promote a cause."，与题干陈述 "Wardian hopes his participation in the seven-day marathon series will contribute to a worthy cause." 信息对应，选 I。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`Wardian is going to join over thirty other runners in a week-long marathon series.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。D 段："He will line up Monday in Antarctica (南极) for the first leg of the World Marathon Challenge—joining 32 other adventure seekers on an unusual journey where participants travel through different time zones and climates to run seven marathons on seven continents…"，与题干陈述 "Wardian is going to join over thirty other runners in a week-long marathon series." 信息对应，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`Over-exertion in extreme cold can lower one's body temperature to a dangerous point.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。请在 A 段中定位与题干陈述对应的信息。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Wardian was very much impressed by a race director's sense of adventure.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`正确答案 F。F 段："Perhaps it was the sense of adventure Donovan displayed when they first met at the 2010 50K Championships in Galway, Ireland, where Donovan was the race director."，与题干陈述 "Wardian was very much impressed by a race director's sense of adventure." 信息对应，选 F。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`Once Wardian sets his mind on something, he is determined to make it happen.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`正确答案 K。K 段：""Once he decides to do it, it's usually something that's going to happen."，与题干陈述 "Once Wardian sets his mind on something, he is determined to make it happen." 信息对应，选 K。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`One top American marathoner quit his running career because of his physical condition.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`正确答案 H。H 段："Hall plans to run with his friend, Pastor Matthew Barnett of The Dream Center in Los Angeles- one of the six American men who will be competing."，与题干陈述 "One top American marathoner quit his running career because of his physical condition." 信息对应，选 H。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`To many of the week-long marathon participants, completing the race will be a success in itself.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`正确答案 I。请在 I 段中定位与题干陈述对应的信息。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`For Wardian, the marathon in the Arctic was more about how to triumph over the extreme cold.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。C 段："The race for Wardian, however, was less about the result than overcoming his aversion to the cold."，与题干陈述 "For Wardian, the marathon in the Arctic was more about how to triumph over the extreme cold." 信息对应，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`To participate in the seven-day marathon series, Wardian had to raise a lot of money and have his wife's support.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`正确答案 G。G 段："Wardian started saving for the trip in 2014, connecting with sponsors and getting approval from his wife, Jennifer, before committing."，与题干陈述 "To participate in the seven-day marathon series, Wardian had to raise a lot of money and have his wife's support." 信息对应，选 G。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say about the pursuit of work-related self-esteem?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It may result in negative motivation.`},{key:`B`,text:`It contributes to one's accomplishments.`},{key:`C`,text:`It can increase one's vigor as one keeps trying.`},{key:`D`,text:`It costs too much emotionally and psychologically.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage One）："Work-related self-esteem is therefore a worthy ideal to pursue with vigor, right?"。正确选项为 "It may result in negative motivation."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What do employees tend to do in pursuing work-related self-esteem?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Take on tasks well beyond their actual capabilities.`},{key:`B`,text:`Strive to succeed at the expense of their well-being.`},{key:`C`,text:`Resort to all means regardless of the consequences.`},{key:`D`,text:`Exaggerate their sense of shame and worthlessness.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage One）："Work-related self-esteem is therefore a worthy ideal to pursue with vigor, right?"。正确选项为 "Strive to succeed at the expense of their well-being."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about people over-concerned with work-related self-esteem?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They may often feel inferior to their colleagues.`},{key:`B`,text:`They cannot enjoy their personal life to the full.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are never satisfied with their achievements.`},{key:`D`,text:`They have their own view of friendship and leisure.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage One）："Work-related self-esteem is therefore a worthy ideal to pursue with vigor, right?"。正确选项为 "They cannot enjoy their personal life to the full."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What is the good news we learn from the recent research?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The pursuit of goals may turn out to be enjoyable and pleasant.`},{key:`B`,text:`The emotional rewards from goal pursuit are worth the pains taken.`},{key:`C`,text:`The negative consequences of goal pursuit can mostly be avoided.`},{key:`D`,text:`The goal of swelling self-esteem can be achieved if one keeps trying.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage One）："According to recent research, in which psychologists interviewed 370 full-time workers over a period of three weeks, the reality is a little more complicated."。正确选项为 "The pursuit of goals may turn out to be enjoyable and pleasant."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What can we infer from the last paragraph?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Workers have to make sacrifices to preserve self-esteem.`},{key:`B`,text:`Self-esteem swells when workers are strongly motivated.`},{key:`C`,text:`Pursuit of goals affects people's personal lives to an unhealthy extent.`},{key:`D`,text:`People feel positive about their work as long as they are motivated.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage One）："However, the way people feel about their work has less to do with whether they're motivated by the preservation of self-esteem but more with the fact that they're simply motivated."。正确选项为 "People feel positive about their work as long as they are motivated."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What is the major finding of the UN environmental study?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Human activity has rendered Mother Earth almost uninhabitable.`},{key:`B`,text:`Humans are doing more damage to the earth than it can cope with.`},{key:`C`,text:`Environmental problems have considerably weakened human prosperity.`},{key:`D`,text:`Environmental damage is more serious in developed countries these days.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage Two）："Degradation of the world's natural resources by humans is rapidly outpacing the planet's ability to absorb the damage, a recent UN environmental study has found."。正确选项为 "Humans are doing more damage to the earth than it can cope with."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What is said about water scarcity in some of the poorest regions?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is getting so serious that there is little hope of a solution.`},{key:`B`,text:`It largely accounts for their slow economic development.`},{key:`C`,text:`It can hardly be relieved if no drastic measures are taken.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is primarily caused by the acceleration of climate change.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage Two）："Water scarcity is the curse of some of the poorest regions on Earth, leaving developing countries increasingly unable to feed themselves, and causing hardship for millions of people."。正确选项为 "It can hardly be relieved if no drastic measures are taken."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What does Achim Steiner say about the environmental condition?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It will deteriorate worldwide.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is attracting global attention.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is being slowly remedied globally.`},{key:`D`,text:`It will shrink the world's population.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage Two）：""If current trends continue, and the world fails to improve patterns of production and consumption, then the state of the world's environment will continue to decline," warned UN executive director Achim Steiner."。正确选项为 "It will deteriorate worldwide."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What is the dilemma developing countries face?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They cannot modernise farming without causing land degradation.`},{key:`B`,text:`They cannot promote industrialisation without polluting waterways.`},{key:`C`,text:`They cannot boost crop yields without causing greenhouse gas emissions.`},{key:`D`,text:`They cannot catch up with rich countries without sacrificing the environment.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage Two）："Water scarcity is the curse of some of the poorest regions on Earth, leaving developing countries increasingly unable to feed themselves, and causing hardship for millions of people."。正确选项为 "They cannot boost crop yields without causing greenhouse gas emissions."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What should developing countries do in their future development according to the passage?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They turn to developed countries for the assistance they need.`},{key:`B`,text:`They remedy environmental damage by slowing economic growth.`},{key:`C`,text:`They avoid damaging interference from international institutions.`},{key:`D`,text:`They improve people's livelihood without harming the environment.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage Two）："But in developing countries, the path of future development has more potential to change, which has encouraged international institutions to devise more sustainable growth pathways that are supposed both to alleviate poverty and preserve the environment."。正确选项为 "They improve people's livelihood without harming the environment."。`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`The Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, located in the southwestern part of China, covers an area of about 2.3 million square kilometers with an average elevation of over 4,000 meters, and is known as the 'Roof of the World'. It is rich in natural resources, boasts beautiful scenery, and is home to a variety of rare wild animals. The plateau has low temperatures, resulting in extensive alpine glaciers. It is the source of many famous rivers in Asia and a primary source of freshwater for China and Southeast Asia. The Qinghai-Tibet Plateau is crucial to the global ecosystem. Due to the impact of climate change, the glaciers on the plateau are melting at an accelerated rate. China has been striving to protect the ecosystem of the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, with increasing grassland coverage and more effective protection for many endangered species.`,guidance:[`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点：`,`1）青藏高原 (the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau)位于中国西南部，面积约 230万平方公里，平均海拔 4 000米以上,被称为"世界屋脊"。 → The Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, located in the southwestern part of China, covers an area of about 2.`,`2）青藏高原自然资源丰富，风景秀丽，拥有多种珍稀野生动物。 → 3 million square kilometers with an average elevation of over 4,000 meters, and is known as the 'Roof of the World'.`,`3）青藏高原气温很低，形成了大面积高山冰川。 → It is rich in natural resources, boasts beautiful scenery, and is home to a variety of rare wild animals.`,`4）这里是亚洲许多著名河流的源头，是中国和东南亚的主要淡水供应源。 → The plateau has low temperatures, resulting in extensive alpine glaciers.`,`5）青藏高原对全球生态系统至关重要。 → It is the source of many famous rivers in Asia and a primary source of freshwater for China and Southeast Asia.`,`6）由于气候变化的影响，青藏高原的冰川正在加速融化。 → The Qinghai-Tibet Plateau is crucial to the global ecosystem.`,`7）中国一直在努力保护青藏高原的生态系统，草地覆盖率不断增加，许多濒危物种得到更为有效的保护。 → Due to the impact of climate change, the glaciers on the plateau are melting at an accelerated rate.`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s1-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`青藏高原 (the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau)位于中国西南部，面积约 230万平方公里，平均海拔 4 000米以上,被称为"世界屋脊"。青藏高原自然资源丰富，风景秀丽，拥有多种珍稀野生动物。 青藏高原气温很低，形成了大面积高山冰川。这里是亚洲许多著名河流的源头，是中国和东南亚的主要淡水供应源。青藏高原对全球生态系统至关重要。 由于气候变化的影响，青藏高原的冰川正在加速融化。中国一直在努力保护青藏高原的生态系统，草地覆盖率不断增加，许多濒危物种得到更为有效的保护。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点： 1）青藏高原 (the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau)位于中国西南部，面积约 230万平方公里，平均海拔 4 000米以上,被称为"世界屋脊"。 → The Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, located in the southwestern part of China, covers an area of about 2. 2）青藏高原自然资源丰富，风景秀丽，拥有多种珍稀野生动物。 → 3 million square kilometers with an average elevation of over 4,000 meters, and is known as the 'Roof of the World'. 3）青藏高原气温很低，形成了大面积高山冰川。 → It is rich in natural resources, boasts beautiful scenery, and is home to a variety of rare wild animals. 4）这里是亚洲许多著名河流的源头，是中国和东南亚的主要淡水供应源。 → The plateau has low temperatures, resulting in extensive alpine glaciers.`,score:15}]}],minutes:125,words:2557,tags:[`真题`,`六级`,`2022`,`12 月`,`第 1 套`],summary:`2022 年 12 月大学英语六级真题（1 套）站内完整版本：写作、听力理解（含逐段原文与站内合成朗读）、阅读理解（选词填空 / 长篇阅读 / 仔细阅读）、翻译全部分区可在线作答，共 57 题，附答案与原文依据解析。`,subtitle:`2022 年 12 月 · 第 1 套 · 站内可作答`},"cet6-2022-12-s2":{parts:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence, "In an era of information explosion, it is vitally important to develop the ability to think critically and make rational choices." You can make statements, give reasons, or cite examples to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`In an era of information explosion, it is vitally important to develop the ability to think critically and make rational choices. The sheer volume of data available can overwhelm individuals, making it challenging to discern truth from falsehood. To navigate this landscape effectively, one must cultivate a discerning mind.
Critical thinking involves analyzing information objectively, questioning assumptions, and drawing logical conclusions. It is not enough to accept information at face value; one must examine the source, the context, and the evidence supporting any claim. For instance, in the realm of social media, where misinformation spreads rapidly, critical thinking is essential to avoid falling prey to fake news. Moreover, rational choices are made when individuals weigh the pros and cons of different options, considering the long-term consequences of their decisions. This is particularly relevant in areas such as finance and health, where the impact of poor choices can be significant.
In conclusion, the ability to think critically and make rational choices is indispensable in today's information-rich society. It empowers individuals to make informed decisions, promotes a culture of intellectual rigor, and safeguards against the pitfalls of misinformation.`,guidance:[`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence, "In an era of information explosion, it is vitally important to develop the ability to think critically and make rati…。参考范文约 186 词、3 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,`范文结构线索：Moreover / In conclusion——这些衔接语标示了论点展开与收束的顺序，可作为自己写作时的段落框架参考。`,`自评建议：对照题目要求逐项检查——是否回应全部提问、段落之间是否有明确逻辑连接、句式与词汇是否匹配话题、词数是否落在规定区间。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence, "In an era of information explosion, it is vitally important to develop the ability to think critically and make rational choices." You can make statements, give reasons, or cite examples to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence, "In an era of information explosion, it is vitally important to develop the ability to think critically and make rati…。参考范文约 186 词、3 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,score:15}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`Welcome to the Book Club. Today's guest is Susan Lane, the author of a new book on personal finances that has already sold half a million copies. Hi, Susan. Your book is extremely successful. Why do you think that is? I think that's because of my message, which is making happiness a priority over money. So many of us in my generation have spent decades trying to earn more money just to consume more, but it made us more miserable. You yourself were once caught in that cycle, working for two decades as an executive and earning a high salary, but still accumulating debt. I most certainly was. I earned millions, but by the time I quit my job four years ago to become a writer, I owed over $30,000. So how did you escape that pattern, and what would you advise other people to do? The first change is in what we value. We need to emphasize things that actually make us happy, like relationships, the environment, or even our hobbies. Once we make the right things our priority, our goals will change, and so will our financial behavior. How does that translate into practical action? Can you give our audience examples of what you're talking about on an everyday basis? The major areas for action are usually housing, food, and transportation. So, people might share a home with friends instead of living on their own, bring lunch from home instead of going to restaurants, and use public transport instead of owning a car. Those sound like major sacrifices. I could never share my home. I need my own space. But they aren't sacrifices. When people change their values, their desires change. So in the example of housing, if we value relationships, sharing a home isn't depriving ourselves of space, but giving us an opportunity to spend more time with our loved ones. Indeed it is.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`Thank you for inviting me to the gallery, Christopher. I haven't visited here since your predecessor's retirement function. Would you like to see the newest additions to our collection first, Catherine? Are those the landscapes by Danielle Gregory? I absolutely adore her work. This first piece was a gift to the gallery from the artist herself, and it's quite exquisite. I love how she depicts the barren landscape. The colors complement each other perfectly. You can sense the desolation in the picture. This piece was inspired by Gregory's recent trek in the Gobi Desert. And how did you obtain her other piece over here? It was purchased at auction by an anonymous collector, who lent it to the gallery for display. This composition is one of her most acclaimed paintings. It must have cost that collector a small fortune to purchase this. Obviously. I can't disclose the exact amount he paid, but it was substantial. There's so much detail in this painting. I feel like I can really immerse myself in the scene. I particularly like the symmetry created by the reflection of the mountain in the lake. This particular piece was the one that was nominated for a Gateway Award. I was lucky enough to attend the award ceremony as Gregory's guest. So, you know her personally. I assume she's an eccentric artist. Quite the opposite, in fact. She's not at all eccentric. I would say she's one of the most easygoing and intelligent people I know. I'd love to be able to meet her. There are so many questions I'd like to ask. What a coincidence. I'm meeting her for dinner tonight. Would you like to come along? I'd love to. Thank you.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-ls-3`,label:`Passage 3`,questionNo:9,text:`When I started high school, it was a shock. I had spent 8 years fighting my way to be the most popular kid in the Catholic school student body. I had been a big, tough 8th grader, and suddenly I was a lowly 9th grader bullied by the big, tough 12th grade seniors who ran the high school. I realized then that it's nice to strive for something, but that you also have to enjoy the moment you're in and be happy where you are. Rock and roll had always been an important part of my life. I remember my friends and I used to drive around until the late hours of the nights listening to the music of rock and roll legends. During those teenage years, I built friendships that I thought would last a lifetime. Most people that age think the same thing, but people drift apart. Jobs, families, and tragedies separate people from those lasting friendships. The tragedy that separated me from my friends forever was the Vietnam War. A year after I graduated from high school, I left for Vietnam. I came back burned out and tired, as though I had lived 10 lifetimes in the short span of 14 months. The 14 months I was in the war, I couldn't relate to the friends I had had in high school. They still seemed childish, concerned with childish things that weren't important to me. I was still trying to cope with the death, destruction, and evil I had seen in Vietnam. I felt like we had done terrible things to innocent people there, and in turn, I had seen terrible things done to my friends. I withdrew from my friends and started college. Then I quit college and took many different jobs. I spent a lot of my time and money on alcohol and other drugs. Finally, in an effort to get my life going in the right direction again, I sold everything and took what little money I had and bought myself an airplane ticket to Israel. I went there to study history. While studying at Haifa University, I met my wife, who was also an American student. I now teach in a high school back in America. I look at my students and see them struggling with many of the very things I struggled with many years ago. As a teacher, I try to help them over the rough spots as best I can.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-ls-4`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:12,text:`Forgiveness is the release of resentment or anger. Forgiveness doesn't mean reconciliation. We don't have to return to the same relationship, nor do we have to accept the same harmful behaviors from an offender. Forgiveness is vitally important for the mental health of certain victims. It propels people forward rather than keeping them emotionally engaged in an injustice or trauma. Carrying the hurt or anger of an offense leads the body to release stress chemicals. Eliminating the perpetual flow of those chemicals may also explain why forgiveness provides physical health benefits. There are scenarios in which forgiveness is not the best course. Sometimes a victim becomes more empowered when they give themselves permission not to forgive. Forgiveness can be challenging. This is especially true when the offending party offers an insincere apology, or maybe they haven't offered anything at all. However, it's often the healthiest path forward. It's important to cultivate forgiveness by developing compassion for the offender. Reflect on whether the act was due to malicious intent, or whether it was caused by challenging circumstances in the offender's life. What about forgiving ourselves? We sometimes need to take responsibility for mistakes, but intense guilt and shame aren't a desirable outcome in the long run. Forgiving yourself may seem like an ambiguous process. You can begin by acknowledging that you are at fault. Take responsibility for the hurt you caused. Then, reflect on why the event occurred. Draw the lessons you learned and try to avoid committing a similar offense in the future. The Glasgow Subway first opened in 1896 as a cable-hauled system. It is generally recognized as the world's third underground railway, after London and Budapest. In its long history, it has never been expanded, remaining as a single loop line with a mere 15 stations. At its peak, it served the shipyard workers on the south side of the city. In the 1960s, there was a decline in the shipbuilding industry, and the popularity of private transport grew. As a result, the subway saw a rapid decline in ridership. It ran with little further change until 1977, when its new operators closed it for major modernization investment. Carriages were replaced, ventilation was improved, and the main depot was also renovated and fitted with connecting tracks to replace the outdated crane transfer mechanism. The subway in its present form reopened for operation in April 1980. Since its relaunch, the subway has seen a revival in its fortunes. It serves as a viable alternative to other forms of transport and has gone a long way to alleviate traffic jams in the city centre. In 1996, the system reached an important milestone, 100 years. To commemorate this special event, the colour scheme of the train carriages was updated. Recently, high-tech systems such as smart card ticketing machines and smart gates are used across all subway stations. The smart card provides more convenient travel, and passengers simply top up their cards and tap them to get in and out the subway. Since its launch, the technology has been adopted by more than 100,000 subway customers.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-ls-5`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:16,text:`If you visit the Great Smoky Mountains National Park, chances are you will remember the roadside or campground bears above all else. Bears are the most popular animals in a number of our national parks. In these mountains, where the population of bears runs into the hundreds, opportunities to observe these large wild animals are plentiful during the summer. Since national parks are wildlife sanctuaries where no disturbance of the native animals is allowed, years of protection have served to break down the wild bears' fear of humans. Now, instead of depending on their own resources for a living, many bears patrol park roads and campgrounds. They give the garbage cans a frequent going -over. An occasional offer of food from a park visitor, an illegal and dangerous practice, makes beggars of them. Bears are very often hungry, and since they will feed on almost any kind of plant or animal, garbage is quite acceptable. Feeding them, however, represents misguided kindness because the bears come to expect such generosity from everyone and, consequently, trouble could lie ahead. Park rules prohibit the feeding of bears. Violators are arrested. Every year, doctors who have offices near the park treat a number of cases of bear bites and bear scratches. Some of the accidents have come about in strange ways. One man was in the process of feeding two small cubs when the mother bear appeared and insisted upon having some of the food. Shoving the big bear aside with one hand, the man continued feeding the cubs when suddenly he was struck a fierce blow in the face. A bear, prompted by the food that a lady kept offering to him, entered the car where the generous person was sitting. Her efforts to push the bear out of the car resulted in injuries. A man required medical attention after he applied a lighted cigarette to a bear's nose. Another man tried to boost a bear into the front seat of his car so that he might take a picture of a bear sitting beside his wife who was behind the wheel. Because bears prefer roads and campgrounds, the possibility of a hiker meeting up with a bear along park trails is small. But there's always that chance, for a bear seems to know if you are carrying a lunch or a candy bar. He may even insist on taking it.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-ls-6`,label:`Passage 2 (2)`,questionNo:19,text:`Why do we form opinions or attitudes about someone or something without really knowing much about them? Just hearing something good or bad about a person, a place or thing can influence our opinions positively or negatively. But letting the opinions of another person determine what our opinions will be is dangerous. Forming opinions about someone or something before really knowing them well is called prejudice. Pre- means before and judice refers to judgment. Hence, prejudice means to judge before having adequate knowledge. We can be prejudiced toward or against someone or something. In either case, we are only allowing ourselves to see half of the picture. Very few people or things in this world are all good or all bad. Prejudiced attitudes are usually based on myths, half -truths, or incorrect information, and they are dangerous because they can keep us from learning the truth about someone or something. People form prejudices against others for many reasons. Differences in their race, religion, gender, or occupation. Prejudices keep people apart. They keep us from really knowing and understanding each other. We should feel proud of who we are and the group of people we represent. If feelings of pride begin to turn to feelings of superiority when we think that our group or our beliefs are better than those around us, however, then we begin to develop prejudiced attitudes that can be harmful. For example, the prejudiced attitudes of one group may keep another group from attending certain schools, from living in any neighborhood they want, or from getting a job or a promotion. Extreme feelings of prejudice have caused the deaths of innocent people. We are responsible for our own thoughts and opinions. When we let someone else tell us what to think about someone or something, we are giving up some control of our own lives. Before you form an attitude or opinion, find out for yourself about the person or the thing in question. Sometimes we don't realize that we hold prejudiced attitudes toward or against someone or something. We need to carefully examine our lives and our fears, and to ask ourselves whether our attitudes come from our personal knowledge and experience or from rumors and fear of the unknown. The good news about prejudice is that we are not born with it. Prejudiced attitudes and opinions develop over time. But with education and knowledge, we can replace our prejudices with cooperation and understanding.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What message does the woman convey in her book?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Prioritizing happiness over money.`},{key:`B`,text:`Joining the club to get her new book.`},{key:`C`,text:`Managing one's personal finances wisely.`},{key:`D`,text:`Consuming more only when earning`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："I think that's because of my message, which is making happiness a priority over money."。正确选项为 "Prioritizing happiness over money."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the woman before she became a writer?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`She was in debt.`},{key:`B`,text:`She was a financial adviser.`},{key:`C`,text:`She earned $ 30,000 a month.`},{key:`D`,text:`She enjoyed a happy life.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "She was in debt."。可在听力原文（Conversation 1）中回听定位。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say about one's financial behavior?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It reflects one's earning power.`},{key:`B`,text:`It varies with one's environment.`},{key:`C`,text:`It mirrors one's sense of wellbeing.`},{key:`D`,text:`It changes with one's goals in life.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："Once we make the right things our priority, our goals will change, and so will our financial behavior."。正确选项为 "It changes with one's goals in life."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about sharing a home?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It would give him more time to be with his loved ones.`},{key:`B`,text:`It would be good for those who value relationships.`},{key:`C`,text:`It would mean major sacrifices for him.`},{key:`D`,text:`It would deprive him of his individuality.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："Those sound like major sacrifices."。正确选项为 "It would mean major sacrifices for him."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about one of the newest additions to the Gallery's collection?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It was the artist's first landscape.`},{key:`B`,text:`It was a painting by Christopher.`},{key:`C`,text:`It was donated by the artist herself.`},{key:`D`,text:`It was displayed at a retirement party.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："Would you like to see the newest additions to our collection first, Catherine?"。正确选项为 "It was donated by the artist herself."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about one of the most acclaimed paintings by Daniel Gregory?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It was the painting that instantly made her rich.`},{key:`B`,text:`It has cost him a lot of money to purchase it.`},{key:`C`,text:`It was recently purchased by the gallery.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is owned by an anonymous collector.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："It was purchased at auction by an anonymous collector, who lent it to the gallery for display."。正确选项为 "It is owned by an anonymous collector."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the woman say she can feel immersed in the scene in the painting?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It reflects her emotions.`},{key:`B`,text:`It contains ample details.`},{key:`C`,text:`It appears perfectly symmetrical.`},{key:`D`,text:`It depicts the beauty of desolation.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："I feel like I can really immerse myself in the scene."。正确选项为 "It contains ample details."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`How does the man describe Daniel Gregory?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`She is eccentric like any other artist.`},{key:`B`,text:`She is a very nice and intelligent artist.`},{key:`C`,text:`She is as lucky as any acclaimed artist.`},{key:`D`,text:`She is one of the most productive artists.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "She is a very nice and intelligent artist."。可在听力原文（Conversation 2）中回听定位。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about forgiveness?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is vital to one's mental health.`},{key:`B`,text:`It leads to reconciliation and peace.`},{key:`C`,text:`It promotes interpersonal relationship.`},{key:`D`,text:`It keeps one from traumatic experience.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "It is vital to one's mental health."。可在听力原文（Passage 3）中回听定位。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`When is forgiveness especially challenging?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`When the offender has power over the victim.`},{key:`B`,text:`When the offender is not willing to apologize.`},{key:`C`,text:`When the offender is not duly penalized`},{key:`D`,text:`When the offender adds insult to injury.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "When the offender is not willing to apologize."。可在听力原文（Passage 3）中回听定位。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What should one do in order to forgive the offender?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Talk with the offender calmly.`},{key:`B`,text:`Accept the offender's apology.`},{key:`C`,text:`Find out why he committed the offense.`},{key:`D`,text:`Determine how serious the offense was.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "Find out why he committed the offense."。可在听力原文（Passage 3）中回听定位。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about the Glasgow subway in the 1960s?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The number of passengers dropped sharply.`},{key:`B`,text:`It served more and more commuters.`},{key:`C`,text:`The number of stations increased to 50.`},{key:`D`,text:`It became the longest in the United Kingdom.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："The Glasgow Subway first opened in 1896 as a cable-hauled system."。正确选项为 "The number of passengers dropped sharply."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`Why was the Glasgow subway closed in 1977?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`To increase capacity to meet growing needs.`},{key:`B`,text:`To make way for other means of transport.`},{key:`C`,text:`To have its systems modernised.`},{key:`D`,text:`To avoid further financial losses.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："The Glasgow Subway first opened in 1896 as a cable-hauled system."。正确选项为 "To have its systems modernised."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the Glasgow subway remain important today?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is generally recognised as a world heritage site.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is the fastest way to reach the city's south side.`},{key:`C`,text:`It constitutes a source of revenue for the city.`},{key:`D`,text:`It helps reduce traffic jams in the city centre.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："It serves as a viable alternative to other forms of transport and has gone a long way to alleviate traffic jams in the city centre."。正确选项为 "It helps reduce traffic jams in the city centre."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about all subway stations in Glasgow nowadays? From the four choices, mark A, B, C, and D.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are usually crowded.`},{key:`B`,text:`They use high-tech systems.`},{key:`C`,text:`They accept smartcards only.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are colourfully decorated.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："Recently, high-tech systems such as smart card ticketing machines and smart gates are used across all subway stations."。正确选项为 "They use high-tech systems."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about the bears in national parks now?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are quite friendly to humans.`},{key:`B`,text:`They are shrinking in numbers.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are unafraid of humans.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are especially fond of garbage.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："Bears are the most popular animals in a number of our national parks."。正确选项为 "They are unafraid of humans."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about visitors feeding bears in national parks?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is strictly forbidden.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is an uncommon sight.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is a gesture of human generosity.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is allowed only in certain areas.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："Bears are the most popular animals in a number of our national parks."。正确选项为 "It is strictly forbidden."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What is the speaker's advice to people who carry some food while hiking on a park trail?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Share their food with the bear they see.`},{key:`B`,text:`Be prepared to run into a hungry bear.`},{key:`C`,text:`Try to be friendly with the bear they meet.`},{key:`D`,text:`Refrain from teasing bears with cubs.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："One man was in the process of feeding two small cubs when the mother bear appeared and insisted upon having some of the food."。正确选项为 "Be prepared to run into a hungry bear."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about prejudice?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It refers to opinions that are radical and widespread.`},{key:`B`,text:`It means making judgments without adequate knowledge.`},{key:`C`,text:`It refers to deep-rooted beliefs about someone or something.`},{key:`D`,text:`It means sticking to one's judgments even when proved wrong.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 2 (2)）依据："Hence, prejudice means to judge before having adequate knowledge."。正确选项为 "It means making judgments without adequate knowledge."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the speaker say prejudiced attitudes are dangerous?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They often lead to war between religious groups.`},{key:`B`,text:`They keep certain occupations from thriving.`},{key:`C`,text:`They allow myths and half-truths to persist.`},{key:`D`,text:`They prevent us from getting to the truth.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 2 (2)）依据："Prejudiced attitudes are usually based on myths, half -truths, or incorrect information, and they are dangerous because they can keep us from learning the truth about someone or something."。正确选项为 "They prevent us from getting to the truth."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`When does the speaker say we begin to develop prejudiced attitudes?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`When we start to feel superior.`},{key:`B`,text:`When we mix with prejudiced people.`},{key:`C`,text:`When we live in an isolated neighborhood.`},{key:`D`,text:`When we try to keep up with those around us.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 2 (2)）依据："If feelings of pride begin to turn to feelings of superiority when we think that our group or our beliefs are better than those around us, however, then we begin to develop prejudiced attitudes that can be harmful."。正确选项为 "When we start to feel superior."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`How did the speaker feel when he started high school?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Motivated.`},{key:`B`,text:`Disappointed.`},{key:`C`,text:`Perplexed.`},{key:`D`,text:`Shocked.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "Shocked."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What did the speaker once think of teenage friendships?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They would change with the passage of time.`},{key:`B`,text:`They would benefit young people's adult life.`},{key:`C`,text:`They would help kids grow.`},{key:`D`,text:`They would last a lifetime.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "They would last a lifetime."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the speaker when he returned from the Vietnam War?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`He had become mature.`},{key:`B`,text:`He suffered poor health.`},{key:`C`,text:`He had lots of stories to tell.`},{key:`D`,text:`He regretted leaving Vietnam.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "He had become mature."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker try to do as a teacher? That's the end of Listening Comprehension.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Make friends with his students.`},{key:`B`,text:`Show his students how to do their best.`},{key:`C`,text:`Help his students get through the growing pains.`},{key:`D`,text:`Share his personal experience with his students.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "Help his students get through the growing pains."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C), and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section. you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`aesthetic`,B:`appropriate`,C:`clusters`,D:`competitive`,E:`exploiting`,F:`fiscal`,G:`instances`,H:`rehabilitated`,I:`reproduced`,J:`revenues`,K:`safeguarding`,L:`ship`,M:`strategic`,N:`transcend`,O:`undergoing`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-cp-1`,text:`The task of the global strategist of a business is to build a platform of capabilities derived from the resources, experiences and innovations of units operating in multiple locations, to transplant those capabilities wherever Apple is an outstanding case of a company whose unique capabilities give it a worldwide By contrast, Telefonica, a Spanish telecommunications company that is now the world's fifth largest telecom by These examples might lead the reader to believe that creating a global advantage is an easy task. But many other`}]},n,{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p1`,title:`Passage 1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p1-1`,text:`Some people in the US have asserted that forgiving student loan debt is one way to stimulate the economy and give assistance to those in need. One government proposition is to eliminate $ 10,000 of debt for 4 economically distressed? students. Some in US Congress have gone so far as to suggest forgiving up to $ 50,000 in debt per student borrower, but does forgiving student debt necessarily correlate to helping the economically disadvantaged?`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p1-2`,text:`The answer is no. This policy is just giving money away to universities and the most affluent students in attendance. Federal Reserve data reveals that the highest-income 40 percent of households owe approximately 60 percent of outstanding student debt, while the lowest 40 percent owe just under 20 percent. This could be due to a combination of factors: students from high-income households are more likely to go to expensive colleges, less likely to receive financial aid, and more likely to have high incomes post-graduation. Plus, the majority of student debt is held by graduate degree earners, who earn approximately 25 percent more than their undergraduate counterparts. Clearly, giving free reign to banks to forgive student debt is a step in the wrong direction.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p1-3`,text:`Other proposals for broader, long-term student loan plans have some fundamental problems. One idea is to cancel student debt only for undergraduate degrees and for students making less than $125,000.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p1-4`,text:`This attempts to address the fact that Congress' previously mentioned student loan forgiveness plan largely helps out the wealthy, but is an adverse incentive for universities to keep raising tuition and for students to choose to major in low-earning degree programs. Colleges have no reason to make their programs more affordable if they believe students will just take out more debt. And, students will feel more comfortable making the irresponsible decision to go tens of thousands of dollars in debt to major in impractical or idealistic subjects if they know their loans will be forgiven.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p1-5`,text:`This is especially concerning given the pandemic (大流行病) has rendered a college education practically worthless. Students are paying tens of thousands of dollars per year to live at home and be lectured on the Internet. Do we really want to tell colleges that they can get away with providing below-average service for an outrageous cost?`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p1-6`,text:`In the case of any of these student debt plans, working-class Americans who chose not to or could not afford to go to college will be subsidizing the education of the professional class. Plumbers and retail workers will be paying for the degrees of doctors and lawyers.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p1-7`,text:`The US government's effort to help those in debt is commendable but is this really the solution that will help the poor financially recover?`}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2`,title:`Passage 2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2-1`,text:`If there's one rule that most parents cling to in the confusing, fast-changing world of kids and media, it's "No screens before age 2 ."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2-2`,text:`As of today, that rule has been thrown out the window.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2-3`,text:`The American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), which first issued that recommendation back in 1999, has extensively updated and revised its guidelines for children and adolescents to reflect new research and new habits.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2-4`,text:`The new guidelines, especially for very young children, shift the focus from WHAT is on the screen to WHO else is in the room. And in doing so, they raise some intriguing points about the future of learning from the media.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2-5`,text:`For babies younger than 18 months, AAP still says no screens at all are the best idea—with one notable exception: Live video chat. Surveys indicate that families already popularly believe that "Face time doesn't count or at least that the benefit of virtual visits with grandparents or other relatives outweighs the potential cost of exposing babies to the laptop or smartphone.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2-6`,text:`The AAP doesn't cite positive evidence that infants actually get something out of this kind of "conversation", the way that they clearly do from live social interaction. But there's some observational research that infants as young as six months old are emotionally engaged by playing live peekaboo (躲猫猫游戏) with Grandma online.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2-7`,text:`For infants and toddlers (学步儿童), ages 15 months to 2 years old, there's limited evidence from a couple of very small studies that they can learn new words from educational media, if and only if parents are watching alongside them, repeating what the video says and/or drawing attention to what is on the screen. In other words, treating a video or an app like a picture book is the best bet.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2-8`,text:`The flip side of this is that many studies have actually shown poorer language skills correlated with earlier solo viewing of "educational" videos. There's also research that shows language delays in children who watch more TV and start watching earlier. In both cases, the problem seems to be media replacing interaction with people. For this reason, the new AAP guideline has changed from "avoid all screens under age 2" to "avoid solo media use in this age group."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-p2-9`,text:`For preschoolers ages 2 to 5, there's more evidence that they have the ability to transfer knowledge from screens to the real world, including early literacy and math. For this age group, AAP recommends no more than an hour a day of screen use. And, just as with younger children, they want care-givers to take part in screen time: "Co-view with your children, help children understand what they are seeing, and help them apply what they learn to the world around them."`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`B`,explanation:`答案 B（appropriate）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`D`,explanation:`答案 D（competitive）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`A`,explanation:`答案 A（aesthetic）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`L`,explanation:`答案 L（ship）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 L。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`I`,explanation:`答案 I（reproduced）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 I。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`J`,explanation:`答案 J（revenues）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 J。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`O`,explanation:`答案 O（undergoing）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 O。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`G`,explanation:`答案 G（instances）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 G。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`M`,explanation:`答案 M（strategic）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 M。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`E`,explanation:`答案 E（exploiting）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`A new study found parents' aversion to certain animals would pass on to their children.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`正确答案 F。F 段："Although the children's reactions were somewhat expected, the new study did contain an unexpected finding: Many of the surveyed children revealed that their parents also expressed fear or disgust of the same animals."，与题干陈述 "A new study found parents' aversion to certain animals would pass on to their children." 信息对应，选 F。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`The disappearance of species and ecological systems erodes our ability to keep what is left.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。A 段："Increasingly, research has shown that as species and ecosystems vanish, it also chips away at our ability to preserve what remains -- because we no longer understand what we're losing."，与题干陈述 "The disappearance of species and ecological systems erodes our ability to keep what is left." 信息对应，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`A study showed that the wildest areas of Discovery Park appealed most to the public.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`正确答案 J。J 段："Published in the journal Frontiers in Sustainable Cities, the study examined attitudes toward Discovery Park, the heavily forested 534-acre public park in Seattle, Washington."，与题干陈述 "A study showed that the wildest areas of Discovery Park appealed most to the public." 信息对应，选 J。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`The fear of living organisms is becoming more worrisome.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。C 段："At its worst it becomes something even more concerning: "biophobia," the fear of living things and a complete aversion to nature."，与题干陈述 "The fear of living organisms is becoming more worrisome." 信息对应，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Preventing the increase in children's fear of living creatures is also important for conserving biodiversity.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`正确答案 G。G 段："Although many conservation biologists still consider that preventing the loss of wildlife habitat is the most important way to conserve biodiversity, I think preventing increased biophobia is also important for conservation."，与题干陈述 "Preventing the increase in children's fear of living creatures is also important for conserving biodiversity." 信息对应，选 G。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`Research shows that more and deeper experience people have with nature will help save it.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`P`,explanation:`正确答案 P。P 段："No matter what techniques we use, this growing field of research illustrates that saving nature requires encouraging people to experience it more often and more deeply."，与题干陈述 "Research shows that more and deeper experience people have with nature will help save it." 信息对应，选 P。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Though humans naturally tend to avoid dangerous animals, today's children try to stay away from even harmless ones.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`正确答案 E。E 段：""Humans inherently avoid dangerous organisms such as bees, but children these days avoid even harmless insects such as butterflies and dragonflies (蜻蜓),"he says."，与题干陈述 "Though humans naturally tend to avoid dangerous animals, today's children try to stay away from even harmless ones." 信息对应，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`Development in and around Discovery Park could cause heavy losses to the park and the local residents.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`M`,explanation:`正确答案 M。M 段："If anything like that happened, both the park and the people of Seattle could lose something vital."，与题干陈述 "Development in and around Discovery Park could cause heavy losses to the park and the local residents." 信息对应，选 M。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`A large survey of school children found that their negative feelings grew as their experience with nature diminished.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。D 段："The less experience the students had with nature, the more negative their feelings."，与题干陈述 "A large survey of school children found that their negative feelings grew as their experience with nature diminished." 信息对应，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Elizabeth Lev believes increased contact with more wildlife helps conserve biodiversity.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`N`,explanation:`正确答案 N。请在 N 段中定位与题干陈述对应的信息。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`Why do some people advocate forgiving student loan debt?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They assert it will narrow the gap between the wealthy and the poor.`},{key:`B`,text:`They believe it will benefit both the economy and the underprivileged.`},{key:`C`,text:`They claim it will eliminate economic distress among college students.`},{key:`D`,text:`They think the cost of education is the responsibility of the government.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage 1）："Some people in the US have asserted that forgiving student loan debt is one way to stimulate the economy and give assistance to those in need."。正确选项为 "They believe it will benefit both the economy and the underprivileged."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the Federal Reserve data?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Approximately 60% of student debt remains unpaid.`},{key:`B`,text:`Cancelling student debt benefits wealthy families most.`},{key:`C`,text:`Forgiving student debt provides little benefit to universities.`},{key:`D`,text:`Low-income families owe the biggest amount of student debt.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage 1）："Federal Reserve data reveals that the highest-income 40 percent of households owe approximately 60 percent of outstanding student debt, while the lowest 40 percent owe just under 20 percent."。正确选项为 "Cancelling student debt benefits wealthy families most."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say students are likely to do if they know they needn't repay their loans?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They will choose to study subjects without considering their job prospects.`},{key:`B`,text:`They will be free to pursue their goals without being burdened financially.`},{key:`C`,text:`They will over-borrow and live beyond their means.`},{key:`D`,text:`They will be able to enroll in expensive universities.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage 1）："And, students will feel more comfortable making the irresponsible decision to go tens of thousands of dollars in debt to major in impractical or idealistic subjects if they know their loans will be forgiven."。正确选项为 "They will choose to study subjects without considering their job prospects."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author imply about colleges offering online education?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They cannot get away with the serious consequences.`},{key:`B`,text:`They have suffered greatly from the current pandemic.`},{key:`C`,text:`The tuition they charge is not justified by the quality of their service.`},{key:`D`,text:`The tuition they charge has surged outrageously during the pandemic.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage 1）："Do we really want to tell colleges that they can get away with providing below-average service for an outrageous cost?"。正确选项为 "The tuition they charge is not justified by the quality of their service."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What will happen if any of the proposed student debt plans is implemented?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Plumbers and retail workers will have a chance of becoming professionals.`},{key:`B`,text:`Working-class students will have increasing access to subsidized education.`},{key:`C`,text:`Blue-collar workers will have to bear the cost of educating would-be high-earners.`},{key:`D`,text:`A growing number of students will be able to earn degrees in medicine and law.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage 1）："In the case of any of these student debt plans, working-class Americans who chose not to or could not afford to go to college will be subsidizing the education of the professional class."。正确选项为 "Blue-collar workers will have to bear the cost of educating would-be high-earners."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the "No screens under 2" rule?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It has met more and more resistance from parents.`},{key:`B`,text:`It has proved helpful to children's healthy growth.`},{key:`C`,text:`It confuses parents with regard to kids' education.`},{key:`D`,text:`It has been discarded in line with recent research.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage 2）："If there's one rule that most parents cling to in the confusing, fast-changing world of kids and media, it's "No screens before age 2 ."。正确选项为 "It has been discarded in line with recent research."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What do the new AAP guidelines advocate?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Young children should be accompanied by parents during screen time.`},{key:`B`,text:`Parents should be emotionally involved in their children's upbringing.`},{key:`C`,text:`Young children should watch videos carefully selected by parents.`},{key:`D`,text:`Parents should protect their children from too much media exposure.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage 2）："The new guidelines, especially for very young children, shift the focus from WHAT is on the screen to WHO else is in the room."。正确选项为 "Young children should be accompanied by parents during screen time."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What do families think of live video chat according to surveys?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It should not be regarded as screen time.`},{key:`B`,text:`It helps babies to develop their verbal skills.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is not as harmful as playing games on laptops.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is a good substitute for video viewing.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage 2）："For babies younger than 18 months, AAP still says no screens at all are the best idea—with one notable exception: Live video chat."。正确选项为 "It should not be regarded as screen time."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What do researchers find about kids solo viewing educational videos?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It arouses their interest in language learning.`},{key:`B`,text:`It works no better than reading picture books.`},{key:`C`,text:`It hampers their development of language skills.`},{key:`D`,text:`It helps them acquire independent learning skills.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage 2）："The flip side of this is that many studies have actually shown poorer language skills correlated with earlier solo viewing of "educational" videos."。正确选项为 "It hampers their development of language skills."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say about preschoolers ages 2 to 5?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They can understand pretty well what they see on the screen.`},{key:`B`,text:`They can learn on their own without much parental guidance.`},{key:`C`,text:`They can make use of educational videos to develop digital literacy.`},{key:`D`,text:`They can relate what they learn on the screen to real life situations.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage 2）："For infants and toddlers (学步儿童), ages 15 months to 2 years old, there's limited evidence from a couple of very small studies that they can learn new words from educational media, if and only if parents are watching alongside them, repeating what the video says…"。正确选项为 "They can relate what they learn on the screen to real life situations."。`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`The Loess Plateau is the third largest plateau in China, covering an area of about 600,000 square kilometers with an average elevation of 1000-2000 meters. The vast majority of it is covered with loess soil that is 50-80 meters thick, making it the region with the most concentrated distribution and the thickest coverage of loess in the world. This is a miracle created by nature, and it is unique in the world. The Loess Plateau is one of the cradles of the Chinese nation. As early as 5500 years ago, people had already begun farming on the Loess Plateau. With the continuous development of agriculture, the population of the Loess Plateau kept increasing, and it became the political and economic center of China during the Qin and Han dynasties. Today, with the implementation of the Western Development Strategy, the economy of the Loess Plateau region has developed rapidly.`,guidance:[`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点：`,`1）黄土高原(the Loess Plateau)是中国第三大高原，面积约60万平方公里，平均海拔1000—2000米，绝大部分覆盖着50—80米厚的黄土，是世界上黄土分布最集中、覆盖… → The Loess Plateau is the third largest plateau in China, covering an area of about 600,000 square kilometers with an average elevation of 1000-2000 me…`,`2）这是大自然创造的一个奇迹，在世界上也是绝无仅有的。 → The vast majority of it is covered with loess soil that is 50-80 meters thick, making it the region with the most concentrated distribution and the th…`,`3）黄土高原是中华民族的发祥地之一。 → This is a miracle created by nature, and it is unique in the world.`,`4）早在5500年前，人们就已经在黄土高原上开始农耕。 → The Loess Plateau is one of the cradles of the Chinese nation.`,`5）随着农耕业的持续发展，黄土高原人口不断增加，在秦汉时期就成为中国的政治和经济中心。 → As early as 5500 years ago, people had already begun farming on the Loess Plateau.`,`6）如今，随着西部大开发战略的实施，黄土高原地区的经济得到了迅速发展。 → With the continuous development of agriculture, the population of the Loess Plateau kept increasing, and it became the political and economic center o…`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s2-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`黄土高原(the Loess Plateau)是中国第三大高原，面积约60万平方公里，平均海拔1000—2000米，绝大部分覆盖着50—80米厚的黄土，是世界上黄土分布最集中、覆盖厚度最大的区域。这是大自然创造的一个奇迹，在世界上也是绝无仅有的。 黄土高原是中华民族的发祥地之一。早在5500年前，人们就已经在黄土高原上开始农耕。随着农耕业的持续发展，黄土高原人口不断增加，在秦汉时期就成为中国的政治和经济中心。如今，随着西部大开发战略的实施，黄土高原地区的经济得到了迅速发展。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点： 1）黄土高原(the Loess Plateau)是中国第三大高原，面积约60万平方公里，平均海拔1000—2000米，绝大部分覆盖着50—80米厚的黄土，是世界上黄土分布最集中、覆盖… → The Loess Plateau is the third largest plateau in China, covering an area of about 600,000 square kilometers with an average elevation of 1000-2000 me… 2）这是大自然创造的一个奇迹，在世界上也是绝无仅有的。 → The vast majority of it is covered with loess soil that is 50-80 meters thick, making it the region with the most concentrated distribution and the th… 3）黄土高原是中华民族的发祥地之一。 → This is a miracle created by nature, and it is unique in the world. 4）早在5500年前，人们就已经在黄土高原上开始农耕。 → The Loess Plateau is one of the cradles of the Chinese nation.`,score:15}]}],minutes:125,words:2643,tags:[`真题`,`六级`,`2022`,`12 月`,`第 2 套`],summary:`2022 年 12 月大学英语六级真题（2 套）站内完整版本：写作、听力理解（含逐段原文与站内合成朗读）、阅读理解（选词填空 / 长篇阅读 / 仔细阅读）、翻译全部分区可在线作答，共 57 题，附答案与原文依据解析。`,subtitle:`2022 年 12 月 · 第 2 套 · 站内可作答`},"cet6-2022-12-s3":{parts:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Today increasing importance is being attached to cultivating college student team spirit." You can make statements, give reasons, or cite examples to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`Today increasing importance is being attached to cultivating college student team spirit. This trend is not only necessary but also vital for the holistic development of students. Firstly, teamwork fosters a sense of belonging and cooperation, which are essential in both academic and professional settings. Students who work in teams learn to communicate effectively, respect diverse opinions, and solve problems collectively. Moreover, engaging in group activities helps students to develop leadership skills and enhances their ability to work under pressure. These skills are indispensable in today's competitive job market. For example, a group research project requires members to divide tasks, exchange evidence, and resolve disagreements before presenting a shared conclusion. Teachers can support this process by assessing both the final result and each member’s contribution. In conclusion, nurturing team spirit among college students is crucial for their future success. It not only prepares them for the challenges of the modern workplace but also instills a sense of community and shared responsibility.`,guidance:[`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Today increasing importance is being attached to cultivating college student team spirit." You can make statements, g…。参考范文约 161 词、1 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,`范文结构线索：Firstly / Moreover / In conclusion——这些衔接语标示了论点展开与收束的顺序，可作为自己写作时的段落框架参考。`,`自评建议：对照题目要求逐项检查——是否回应全部提问、段落之间是否有明确逻辑连接、句式与词汇是否匹配话题、词数是否落在规定区间。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Today increasing importance is being attached to cultivating college student team spirit." You can make statements, give reasons, or cite examples to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Today increasing importance is being attached to cultivating college student team spirit." You can make statements, g…。参考范文约 161 词、1 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,score:15}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）；本套听力原文暂未收录。`,segments:[]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What message does the woman convey in her book?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Prioritizing happiness over money.`},{key:`B`,text:`Joining the club to get her new book.`},{key:`C`,text:`Managing one's personal finances wisely.`},{key:`D`,text:`Consuming more only when earning`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "Prioritizing happiness over money."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the woman before she became a writer?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`She was in debt.`},{key:`B`,text:`She was a financial adviser.`},{key:`C`,text:`She earned $ 30,000 a month.`},{key:`D`,text:`She enjoyed a happy life.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "She was in debt."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say about one's financial behavior?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It reflects one's earning power.`},{key:`B`,text:`It varies with one's environment.`},{key:`C`,text:`It mirrors one's sense of wellbeing.`},{key:`D`,text:`It changes with one's goals in life.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "It changes with one's goals in life."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about sharing a home?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It would give him more time to be with his loved ones.`},{key:`B`,text:`It would be good for those who value relationships.`},{key:`C`,text:`It would mean major sacrifices for him.`},{key:`D`,text:`It would deprive him of his individuality.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "It would mean major sacrifices for him."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about one of the newest additions to the Gallery's collection?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It was the artist's first landscape.`},{key:`B`,text:`It was a painting by Christopher.`},{key:`C`,text:`It was donated by the artist herself.`},{key:`D`,text:`It was displayed at a retirement party.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "It was donated by the artist herself."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about one of the most acclaimed paintings by Daniel Gregory?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It was the painting that instantly made her rich.`},{key:`B`,text:`It has cost him a lot of money to purchase it.`},{key:`C`,text:`It was recently purchased by the gallery.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is owned by an anonymous collector.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "It is owned by an anonymous collector."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the woman say she can feel immersed in the scene in the painting?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It reflects her emotions.`},{key:`B`,text:`It contains ample details.`},{key:`C`,text:`It appears perfectly symmetrical.`},{key:`D`,text:`It depicts the beauty of desolation.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "It contains ample details."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`How does the man describe Daniel Gregory?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`She is eccentric like any other artist.`},{key:`B`,text:`She is a very nice and intelligent artist.`},{key:`C`,text:`She is as lucky as any acclaimed artist.`},{key:`D`,text:`She is one of the most productive artists.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "She is a very nice and intelligent artist."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about forgiveness?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is vital to one's mental health.`},{key:`B`,text:`It leads to reconciliation and peace.`},{key:`C`,text:`It promotes interpersonal relationship.`},{key:`D`,text:`It keeps one from traumatic experience.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "It is vital to one's mental health."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`When is forgiveness especially challenging?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`When the offender has power over the victim.`},{key:`B`,text:`When the offender is not willing to apologize.`},{key:`C`,text:`When the offender is not duly penalized`},{key:`D`,text:`When the offender adds insult to injury.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "When the offender is not willing to apologize."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What should one do in order to forgive the offender?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Talk with the offender calmly.`},{key:`B`,text:`Accept the offender's apology.`},{key:`C`,text:`Find out why he committed the offense.`},{key:`D`,text:`Determine how serious the offense was.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "Find out why he committed the offense."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about the Glasgow subway in the 1960s?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The number of passengers dropped sharply.`},{key:`B`,text:`It served more and more commuters.`},{key:`C`,text:`The number of stations increased to 50.`},{key:`D`,text:`It became the longest in the United Kingdom.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "The number of passengers dropped sharply."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`Why was the Glasgow subway closed in 1977?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`To increase capacity to meet growing needs.`},{key:`B`,text:`To make way for other means of transport.`},{key:`C`,text:`To have its systems modernised.`},{key:`D`,text:`To avoid further financial losses.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "To have its systems modernised."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the Glasgow subway remain important today?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is generally recognised as a world heritage site.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is the fastest way to reach the city's south side.`},{key:`C`,text:`It constitutes a source of revenue for the city.`},{key:`D`,text:`It helps reduce traffic jams in the city centre.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "It helps reduce traffic jams in the city centre."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about all subway stations in Glasgow nowadays? From the four choices, mark A, B, C, and D.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are usually crowded.`},{key:`B`,text:`They use high-tech systems.`},{key:`C`,text:`They accept smartcards only.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are colourfully decorated.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "They use high-tech systems."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about the bears in national parks now?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are quite friendly to humans.`},{key:`B`,text:`They are shrinking in numbers.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are unafraid of humans.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are especially fond of garbage.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "They are unafraid of humans."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about visitors feeding bears in national parks?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is strictly forbidden.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is an uncommon sight.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is a gesture of human generosity.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is allowed only in certain areas.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "It is strictly forbidden."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What is the speaker's advice to people who carry some food while hiking on a park trail?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Share their food with the bear they see.`},{key:`B`,text:`Be prepared to run into a hungry bear.`},{key:`C`,text:`Try to be friendly with the bear they meet.`},{key:`D`,text:`Refrain from teasing bears with cubs.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "Be prepared to run into a hungry bear."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say about prejudice?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It refers to opinions that are radical and widespread.`},{key:`B`,text:`It means making judgments without adequate knowledge.`},{key:`C`,text:`It refers to deep-rooted beliefs about someone or something.`},{key:`D`,text:`It means sticking to one's judgments even when proved wrong.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "It means making judgments without adequate knowledge."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the speaker say prejudiced attitudes are dangerous?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They often lead to war between religious groups.`},{key:`B`,text:`They keep certain occupations from thriving.`},{key:`C`,text:`They allow myths and half-truths to persist.`},{key:`D`,text:`They prevent us from getting to the truth.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "They prevent us from getting to the truth."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`When does the speaker say we begin to develop prejudiced attitudes?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`When we start to feel superior.`},{key:`B`,text:`When we mix with prejudiced people.`},{key:`C`,text:`When we live in an isolated neighborhood.`},{key:`D`,text:`When we try to keep up with those around us.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "When we start to feel superior."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`How did the speaker feel when he started high school?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Motivated.`},{key:`B`,text:`Disappointed.`},{key:`C`,text:`Perplexed.`},{key:`D`,text:`Shocked.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "Shocked."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What did the speaker once think of teenage friendships?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They would change with the passage of time.`},{key:`B`,text:`They would benefit young people's adult life.`},{key:`C`,text:`They would help kids grow.`},{key:`D`,text:`They would last a lifetime.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "They would last a lifetime."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about the speaker when he returned from the Vietnam War?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`He had become mature.`},{key:`B`,text:`He suffered poor health.`},{key:`C`,text:`He had lots of stories to tell.`},{key:`D`,text:`He regretted leaving Vietnam.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "He had become mature."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker try to do as a teacher? That's the end of Listening Comprehension.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Make friends with his students.`},{key:`B`,text:`Show his students how to do their best.`},{key:`C`,text:`Help his students get through the growing pains.`},{key:`D`,text:`Share his personal experience with his students.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "Help his students get through the growing pains."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C), and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section. you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`abandoned`,B:`access`,C:`consumption`,D:`contrive`,E:`duplications`,F:`investments`,G:`mobilized`,H:`negligent`,I:`neutral`,J:`reductions`,K:`released`,L:`replacement`,M:`slash`,N:`surplus`,O:`void`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-cp-1`,text:`American colleges and universities are using 64 percent less coal than they did a decade ago, burning 700,000 tons last year, down from 2 million tons in 2008, the U. S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) said in a report All 57 schools that were burning coal in 2008 are using less now, and 20 have Most universities have turned to natural gas as a While academic institutions use less than 0.1 percent of U.S. coal burned for power, campus coal use has a history dating back to the 1800s when Many universities still operate their own power plants. The Public Utility Regulatory Policies Act of 1978 encouraged more electricity generation by allowing institutions to sell But EIA noted many coal-fired universities have signed onto the American College and University Presidents Climate Commitment, which was launched in 2007. About 665 schools are part of the program, which aims to The Sierra Club's Beyond Coal campaign, which also leads campaigns for universities to withdraw their The largest coal use During the same period, Michigan made an 80 percent cut and Tennessee cut back by 94 percent at state institutions.`}]},r,{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p1`,title:`Passage One`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p1-1`,text:`How can one person enjoy good health, while another person looks old before her time? Humans have been asking this question for thousands of years, and recently, it's becoming clearer and clearer to scientists that the differences between people's rates of aging lie in the complex interactions among genes, social relationships, environments and lifestyles. Even though you were born with a particular set of genes, the way you live can influence how they express themselves. Some lifestyle factors may even turn genes on or shut them off.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p1-2`,text:`Deep within the genetic heart of all our cells are telomeres, or repeating segments of noncoding DNA that live at the ends of the chromosomes (染色体). They form caps at the ends of the chromosomes and keep the genetic material together. Shortening with each cell division, they help determine how fast a cell ages. When they become too short, the cell stops dividing altogether. This isn't the only reason a cell can age—there are other stresses on cells we don't yet understand very well, but short telomeres are one of the major reasons human cells grow old. We've devoted most of our careers to studying telomeres, and one extraordinary discovery from our labs is that telomeres can actually lengthen.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p1-3`,text:`Scientists have learned that several thought patterns appear to be unhealthy for telomeres, and one of them is cynical hostility. Cynical hostility is defined by high anger and frequent thoughts that other people cannot be trusted. Someone with hostility doesn't just think, "I hate to stand in long lines"; they think, "Others deliberately sped up and beat me to my rightful position in the line!" and then get violently agitated. People who score high on measures of cynical hostility tend to get more heart disease, metabolic disease and often die at younger ages. They also have shorter telomeres. In a study of British civil servants, men who scored high on measures of cynical hostility had shorter telomeres than men whose hostility scores were low. The most hostile men were 30% more likely to have short telomeres.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p1-4`,text:`What this means: aging is a dynamic process that could possibly be accelerated or slowed—and, in some aspects, even reversed. To an extent, it has surprised us and the rest of the scientific community that telomeres do not simply carry out the commands issued by your genetic code. Your telomeres are listening to you. The foods you eat, your response to challenges, the amount of exercise you get, and many other factors appear to influence your telomeres and can prevent premature aging at the cellular level. One of the keys to enjoying good health is simply doing your part to foster healthy cell renewal.`}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2`,title:`Passage Two`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2-1`,text:`Scientists have created by accident an enzyme (酶) that breaks down plastic drinks bottles. The breakthrough could help solve the global plastic pollution crisis by enabling for the first time the full recycling of bottles.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2-2`,text:`The new research was spurred by the discovery in 2016 of the first bacterium that had naturally evolved to eat plastic at a waste dump in Japan. Scientists have now revealed the detailed structure of the crucial enzyme produced by the bug.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2-3`,text:`An international team then adjusted the enzyme to see how it had evolved, but tests showed they had accidentally made the molecule even better at breaking down the plastic used for drinks bottles. "What actually turned out was we improved the enzyme, which was a bit of a shock," said head researcher Prof. McGeehan, at the University of Portsmouth, UK.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2-4`,text:`Currently, the enzyme takes a few days to start breaking down the plastic, far faster than the centuries it takes in the oceans, but the researchers are optimistic this can be speeded up even further and become a viable large-scale process.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2-5`,text:`"What we are hoping to do is use this enzyme to turn this plastic back into its original components, so we can literally recycle it back to plastic," said McGeehan. "It means we won't need to dig up any more oil and, fundamentally, it should reduce the amount of plastic in the environment."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2-6`,text:`About 1 million plastic bottles are sold each minute around the globe and, with just 14% recycled, many end up in the oceans where they have polluted even the remotest parts, harming marine life and potentially people who eat seafood. "Plastic is incredibly resistant to degradation," said McGeehan. "It is one of these wonder materials that has been made a little bit too well."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2-7`,text:`Currently those bottles that are recycled can only be turned into opaque fibres for clothing or carpets, while the new enzyme indicates a way to recycle old clear plastic bottles back into new clear plastic bottles.`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2-8`,text:`"You are always up against the fact oil is cheap, so plastic is cheap," said McGeehan. "It is so easy for manufacturers to generate more of that stuff, rather than even try to recycle, but I believe there is a public interest here: perception is changing so much that companies are starting to look at how they can properly recycle these bottles."`},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-p2-9`,text:`Prof. Adisa Azapagic, at the University of Manchester in the UK, agreed the enzyme could be useful but added: "A full life-cycle assessment would be needed to ensure that the technology does not solve one environmental problem- waste- at the expense of others, including additional greenhouse gas emissions."`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`K`,explanation:`答案 K（released）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 K。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`A`,explanation:`答案 A（abandoned）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`L`,explanation:`答案 L（replacement）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 L。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`B`,explanation:`答案 B（access）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`N`,explanation:`答案 N（surplus）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 N。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`M`,explanation:`答案 M（slash）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 M。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`I`,explanation:`答案 I（neutral）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 I。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`F`,explanation:`答案 F（investments）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 F。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`J`,explanation:`答案 J（reductions）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 J。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`C`,explanation:`答案 C（consumption）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`It was not a rare occurrence that audiences behaved wildly while listening to classical music.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`正确答案 E。E 段："The truth is that classical music audiences weren't always so polite."，与题干陈述 "It was not a rare occurrence that audiences behaved wildly while listening to classical music." 信息对应，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`Some high-art institutions don't actually mean it when they say they want more general audiences.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`L`,explanation:`正确答案 L。L 段：""On one hand, these organizations are all saying the same thing: we want more general audiences, to break down cultural barriers," he said."，与题干陈述 "Some high-art institutions don't actually mean it when they say they want more general audiences." 信息对应，选 L。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`The theatre was in chaos when an unconventional ballet was first put on stage in the capital of France.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。A 段："Igor Stravinsky was introducing his revolutionary "Rite of Spring" ballet to the world, with its discordant melodies and unorthodox choreography (编舞), and the purists in the crowd expressed their disapproval loud and clear."，与题干陈述 "The theatre was in chaos when an unconventional ballet was first put on stage in the capital of France." 信息对应，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`According to one critic, the audience's warm response would encourage the musicians to do a better job.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`M`,explanation:`正确答案 M。M 段："He suggested that the warmer an audience is, the better the musicians themselves will respond."，与题干陈述 "According to one critic, the audience's warm response would encourage the musicians to do a better job." 信息对应，选 M。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Many commenters argued for the audience enjoying classical music quietly.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。D 段："For example, should we feel OK "clapping" during classical music events, even if nobody else is?"，与题干陈述 "Many commenters argued for the audience enjoying classical music quietly." 信息对应，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`What appears on the seatback screen makes it unnecessary for the audience to study the works beforehand.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`正确答案 H。H 段："When operas first instituted subtitles (字幕) during shows, he said, many purists didn't like the idea, believing that the audience should instead study the works before attending."，与题干陈述 "What appears on the seatback screen makes it unnecessary for the audience to study the works beforehand." 信息对应，选 H。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`It is generally accepted that there should be no disturbance from the audience during classical music performance.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。B 段："It's difficult to imagine a similar disturbance occurring today within America's sacred symphony halls."，与题干陈述 "It is generally accepted that there should be no disturbance from the audience during classical music performance." 信息对应，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`Higher institutions will be concerned about compromising the integrity of classical music if they have to resort to the television medium.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`O`,explanation:`正确答案 O。O 段："And if they appeal to these outlets, do they risk compromising the integrity or the intelligence of the music?"，与题干陈述 "Higher institutions will be concerned about compromising the integrity of classical music if they have to resort to the television medium." 信息对应，选 O。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`Heavily discounted rush tickets help attract many young students to attend classical concerts.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`正确答案 K。K 段："The orchestra also continues to offer $25 annual memberships to Philadelphia students, who can buy rush tickets to every concert on the schedule."，与题干陈述 "Heavily discounted rush tickets help attract many young students to attend classical concerts." 信息对应，选 K。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`The formalities of high-art theatres can intimidate some people attending a performance.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`N`,explanation:`正确答案 N。N 段："Certain people "think they have to dress up," he wrote."，与题干陈述 "The formalities of high-art theatres can intimidate some people attending a performance." 信息对应，选 N。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What have scientists come to know better today?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Why people age at different rates.`},{key:`B`,text:`How genes influence the aging process.`},{key:`C`,text:`How various genes express themselves in aging.`},{key:`D`,text:`Why people have long been concerned about aging.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage One）："Humans have been asking this question for thousands of years, and recently, it's becoming clearer and clearer to scientists that the differences between people's rates of aging lie in the complex interactions among genes, social relationships, environments and…"。正确选项为 "Why people age at different rates."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`Why are some lifestyle factors considered extremely important?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They may shorten the process of cell division.`},{key:`B`,text:`They may determine how genes function.`},{key:`C`,text:`They may affect the lifespan of telomeres.`},{key:`D`,text:`They may account for the stresses on cells.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage One）："Some lifestyle factors may even turn genes on or shut them off."。正确选项为 "They may determine how genes function."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What have the author and his colleagues discovered about telomeres?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Their number affects the growth of cells.`},{key:`B`,text:`Their length determines the quality of life.`},{key:`C`,text:`Their shortening process can be reversed.`},{key:`D`,text:`Their health impacts the division of cells.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage One）："What this means: aging is a dynamic process that could possibly be accelerated or slowed—and, in some aspects, even reversed."。正确选项为 "Their shortening process can be reversed."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What have scientists learned about cynical hostility?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It may lead to confrontational thought patterns.`},{key:`B`,text:`It may produce an adverse effect on telomeres.`},{key:`C`,text:`It may cause people to lose their temper frequently.`},{key:`D`,text:`It may stir up agitation among those in long lines.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage One）："Scientists have learned that several thought patterns appear to be unhealthy for telomeres, and one of them is cynical hostility."。正确选项为 "It may produce an adverse effect on telomeres."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the last paragraph about the process of aging?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It may vary from individual to individual.`},{key:`B`,text:`It challenges scientists to explore further.`},{key:`C`,text:`It depends on one's genetic code.`},{key:`D`,text:`It may be controlled to a degree.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage One）："What this means: aging is a dynamic process that could possibly be accelerated or slowed—and, in some aspects, even reversed."。正确选项为 "It may be controlled to a degree."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the passage about an enzyme scientists have created?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It was identified during a lab experiment accident.`},{key:`B`,text:`It may make full recycling of plastic bottles a reality.`},{key:`C`,text:`It was a breakthrough made with persistent efforts.`},{key:`D`,text:`It may initiate a radical reform in the plastic industry.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage Two）："Scientists have created by accident an enzyme (酶) that breaks down plastic drinks bottles."。正确选项为 "It may make full recycling of plastic bottles a reality."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about the bug that produces the important enzyme?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It has a natural ability to consume plastics.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is a bacterium that reproduces at a high rate.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is essential to the recycling of plastic bottles.`},{key:`D`,text:`It has a chemical structure unknown to scientists.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "It has a natural ability to consume plastics."。请回原文（）核对细节。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`By adjusting the enzyme produced by the bug, the scientists .`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`made it more effective by chance`},{key:`B`,text:`discovered an extraordinary chemical`},{key:`C`,text:`altered its basic molecular composition`},{key:`D`,text:`found its evolutionary process sped up`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage Two）："Scientists have now revealed the detailed structure of the crucial enzyme produced by the bug."。正确选项为 "made it more effective by chance"。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What does Prof. McGeehan say about the recycling of plastic bottles?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Manufacturers are implementing it on an increasingly larger scale.`},{key:`B`,text:`It generates huge business opportunities for plastic manufacturers.`},{key:`C`,text:`It has aroused persistent interest among the general public.`},{key:`D`,text:`Manufacturers are beginning to explore ways of doing it.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage Two）："The breakthrough could help solve the global plastic pollution crisis by enabling for the first time the full recycling of bottles."。正确选项为 "Manufacturers are beginning to explore ways of doing it."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What is Prof. Adisa Azapagic's advice concerning the application of the enzyme?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Developing technologies to address greenhouse gas emissions.`},{key:`B`,text:`Considering the extra cost involved in producing the enzyme.`},{key:`C`,text:`Assessing its possible negative impact on the environment.`},{key:`D`,text:`Studying the full life cycle of the enzyme as the first step.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage Two）：""What actually turned out was we improved the enzyme, which was a bit of a shock," said head researcher Prof."。正确选项为 "Assessing its possible negative impact on the environment."。`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`The Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau, mostly located within Yunnan and Guizhou provinces, covers an area of about 500,000 square kilometers with an average elevation of 2,000-4,000 meters, making it the fourth largest plateau in China. The plateau slopes from west to east, with numerous rivers that have carved out many deep and steep canyons. The soil in many areas of the canyons is fertile, highly conducive to the growth of a variety of crops.
The unique natural environment of the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau has fostered biodiversity and cultural diversity. It is a region in China with a rich variety of forest and mineral resources, as well as an important area for the origins of ancient humans. The plateau is home to more ethnic minority groups than any other region in China, each preserving their own rich and colorful cultural traditions.`,guidance:[`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点：`,`1）云贵高原(the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau)大部分位于云南、贵州省境内，总面积约50万平方公里，平均海拔 2 000—4 000米，是中国第四大高原。 → The Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau, mostly located within Yunnan and Guizhou provinces, covers an area of about 500,000 square kilometers with an average elev…`,`2）云贵高原西高东低，河流众多，形成了许多又深又陡的峡谷(canyon)。 → The plateau slopes from west to east, with numerous rivers that have carved out many deep and steep canyons.`,`3）峡谷中许多地方土壤肥沃，非常有利于多种农作物生长。 → The soil in many areas of the canyons is fertile, highly conducive to the growth of a variety of crops.`,`4）云贵高原独特的自然环境造就了生物和文化的多样性。 → The unique natural environment of the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau has fostered biodiversity and cultural diversity.`,`5）它是中国森林和矿产资源类型十分丰富的地区，也是古人类起源的重要地区。 → It is a region in China with a rich variety of forest and mineral resources, as well as an important area for the origins of ancient humans.`,`6）云贵高原是中国少数民族数量最多的地区，各民族都保留了自己丰富多彩的文化传统。 → The plateau is home to more ethnic minority groups than any other region in China, each preserving their own rich and colorful cultural traditions.`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2022-12-s3-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`云贵高原(the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau)大部分位于云南、贵州省境内，总面积约50万平方公里，平均海拔 2 000—4 000米，是中国第四大高原。云贵高原西高东低，河流众多，形成了许多又深又陡的峡谷(canyon)。峡谷中许多地方土壤肥沃，非常有利于多种农作物生长。 云贵高原独特的自然环境造就了生物和文化的多样性。它是中国森林和矿产资源类型十分丰富的地区，也是古人类起源的重要地区。云贵高原是中国少数民族数量最多的地区，各民族都保留了自己丰富多彩的文化传统。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点： 1）云贵高原(the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau)大部分位于云南、贵州省境内，总面积约50万平方公里，平均海拔 2 000—4 000米，是中国第四大高原。 → The Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau, mostly located within Yunnan and Guizhou provinces, covers an area of about 500,000 square kilometers with an average elev… 2）云贵高原西高东低，河流众多，形成了许多又深又陡的峡谷(canyon)。 → The plateau slopes from west to east, with numerous rivers that have carved out many deep and steep canyons. 3）峡谷中许多地方土壤肥沃，非常有利于多种农作物生长。 → The soil in many areas of the canyons is fertile, highly conducive to the growth of a variety of crops. 4）云贵高原独特的自然环境造就了生物和文化的多样性。 → The unique natural environment of the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau has fostered biodiversity and cultural diversity.`,score:15}]}],minutes:125,words:2715,tags:[`真题`,`六级`,`2022`,`12 月`,`第 3 套`],summary:`2022 年 12 月大学英语六级真题（3 套）站内完整版本：写作、听力理解（含逐段原文与站内合成朗读）、阅读理解（选词填空 / 长篇阅读 / 仔细阅读）、翻译全部分区可在线作答，共 57 题，附答案与原文依据解析。`,subtitle:`2022 年 12 月 · 第 3 套 · 站内可作答`}};export{i as default};